As shown in Fig. 1, the usage of the m-benzenedicarboxylate subunit for the construction of porous MOFs was initiated by the readiness of the subunit to assemble with paddlewheel $\mathrm{{Cu}_{2}(\mathrm{{COO})_{4}}}$ SBUs to form a porous metal–organic polyhedron (MOP) by $\mathrm{\DeltaYaghi}^{62}$ and Zaworotko63 and a prototype porous MOF HKUST-1 (HKUST represents ‘‘Hong Kong University of Science and Technology’’) by Williams.64 Chen and Schro¨der explored the first series of porous MOFs constructed from $m$ -benzenedicarboxylate subunits and dicopper paddlewheel $\mathrm{{Cu}}_{2}(\mathrm{{COO}})_{4}$ SBUs.65–67 These MOFs not only have the same topologies, but also have systematically tunable porosities for their gas storage. This work immediately attracted wide attention in the MOF community. Apparently, incorporation of these $m$ -benzenedicarboxylate subunits with different aromatic backbones can not only generate a large number of tetracarboxylates but also a variety of hexacarboxylates and octacarboxylates for the construction of porous MOFs.
In this review, we will summarize and highlight multifunctional porous MOFs constructed from these tetra-, hexa- and octacarboxylates consisting of the very basic $m$ -benzenedicarboxylate subunits. Because these porous MOFs have very rich MOF chemistry in terms of their structures, framework topologies, porosities and pore functionalities, their applications in gas storage and separation, heterogeneous catalysis and luminescent sensing have been extensively explored, which have been outlined in this review.
## 2. Multifunctional MOFs based on di-isophthalates
Scheme 1 summarizes the tetracarboxylic acids containing two $m$ -benzenedicarboxylate moieties with different spacers between them, which have been incorporated into porous MOFs. The explored spacers include double bond, triple bond, polyphenyl, chiral binaphthyl, and nitrogen-containing heterocyclic rings. Most diisophthalate organic linkers can be easily prepared by the cross-coupling reactions such as Suzuki coupling, and the Sonogashira coupling reaction, as well as the copper-catalyzed azide–alkyne cycloaddition (CuAAC) reaction.
## 2.1 Gas adsorption
2.1.1 Copper di-isophthalate frameworks. A copper–tetracarboxylate framework MOF-505 was synthesized via a solvothermal reaction of $^{3,3^{\prime},5,5^{\prime}}$ -biphenyltetracarboxylic acid $\mathrm{\big(H_{4}b p t c,}$ $\mathrm{H}_{4}\mathrm{A}1$ , see Scheme 1) and $\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}2.5\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}.$ 65 The rectangular organic building blocks are connected by in situ formed squareplanar dicopper paddlewheel $\mathrm{{Cu}_{2}(\mathrm{{COO})_{4}}}$ SBUs, giving rise to a three-dimensional (3D) structure based on the 4-coordinated nbo net. There exist two different types of polyhedral cages in the resulting framework, one of which is composed of 12 ligands connecting 6 paddlewheel SBUs, and the other one is formed from 6 ligands connecting 12 paddlewheel SBUs. The two cages are connected to each other by sharing three paddlewheel SBUs, and arranged in an alternating fashion in a 1 : 1 ratio, forming a 3D cage-stacking framework. Comparison of the $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ adsorption properties under different activation conditions revealed that the created open copper sites have a favourable impact on the $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ sorption capacity of MOF-505. MOF-505 takes up $2.47~\mathrm{wt\%}$ of $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ at $77~\mathrm{K}$ and 1 bar.
After the pioneering work by Chen et al., the nbo-related MOF-505 analogues derived from linear di-isophthalate linkers and dicopper paddlewheel $\mathrm{{Cu}_{2}(\mathrm{{COO})_{4}}}$ SBUs have gained tremendous attention because catenation is basically difficult in such cage-based MOF networks, and most importantly these types of MOF materials exhibit promising gas sorption properties because of their high surface areas, tunable pore sizes and open copper sites. Currently, further efforts have been devoted to increasing the surface area, optimizing the pore size and immobilizing specific binding sites within the MOF-505 series to maximize their gas storage capacities.
Schro¨der et al.66,67 synthesized a series of copper–tetracarboxylate frameworks (NOTT-100 to NOTT-109; NOTT represents ‘‘University of Nottingham’’), which were formed from a range of polyphenyl tetracarboxylates of varying length and functionalization $\mathbf{\left(H_{4}A1}$ , $\mathrm{H}_{4}\mathrm{A}8–\mathrm{H}_{4}\mathrm{A}12$ , $\mathrm{H}_{4}\mathrm{A}14$ , $\mathbf{H}_{4}\mathbf{A}16$ , $\mathbf{H_{4}A19}_{}$ ) and dicopper paddlewheel $\mathrm{{Cu}}_{2}(\mathrm{{COO}})_{4}$ SBUs, to investigate the role of pore size, linker functionalization, and open metal sites (OMSs) on $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ adsorption. These frameworks have the same nbo-based topology as that of the prototypal framework MOF505 except NOTT-109 (erroneously assigned the pts topology, see Section 5.2) due to the spatial obstacle of the bulky central aromatic groups in the ligand. The pore sizes can be tuned by the polyaromatic backbones. Due to the large pore volumes as well as the presence of open copper sites, all desolvated materials exhibit high total $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ adsorption in the range of $2.24{-}2.63\ \mathrm{\wt\%}$ under 1 bar and $4.02\substack{-6.51\ \mathrm{wt\%}}$ under 20 bar at $77\mathrm{~K~}$ , respectively. In the case of NOTT-107, introducing methyl groups to create smaller pores can increase the heat of $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ adsorption and thus uptake at low pressure; however, it also reduces the free volume and thus negatively affects the highpressure $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ adsorption capacity.
Subsequently, the same group synthesized two more isostructural framework compounds,68 namely NOTT-110 and NOTT-111, via solvothermal reactions of $\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}2.5\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and the corresponding ligands, (2,7-phenanthrenediyl)-diisophthalic acid $\left(\mathrm{H}_{4}\mathbf{A}20\right)$ or (2,7-(9,10-dihydrophenanthrenediyl))diisophthalic acid $\mathbf{\Pi}(\mathbf{H}_{4}\mathbf{A}21)$ , respectively, to investigate the effect of the curvature of the ligand on $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ adsorption. Compared with the tetraphenyl based analogue NOTT-102, the phenanthrene and 9,10-dihydrophenanthrene groups in NOTT-110 and NOTT-111 cover more cage surfaces than the biphenyl groups in NOTT-102, making the cages more compact in NOTT-110 and NOTT-111. Such structural features result in a significant enhancement of $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ adsorption in the low and medium pressure range (0–20 bar). At 77 K and 1 bar, the total $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ uptakes of the desolvated NOTT-110 and NOTT-111 reach 2.64 and $2.56~\mathrm{wt\%}$ , respectively. These values are $18\%$ higher than that of NOTT-102, despite the similar BET surface areas and pore volumes for all desolvated frameworks. At $77\mathrm{~K~}$ and 20 bar, the total $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ uptakes for NOTT110 and NOTT-111 are $6.59\ \mathrm{wt\%}$ and $6.48~\mathrm{wt\%}$ , respectively, which are approximately $8\%$ higher than the capacity of NOTT102. Moreover, NOTT-110 shows a high total $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ storage capacity of $7.62~\mathrm{wt\%}$ at $77~\mathrm{K}$ and 55 bar. This study indicates that the curved ligand incorporated into a MOF can improve $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ adsorption because it provides a pocket facilitating gas adsorption.
Suh et al. used $^{1,1^{\prime}}$ -azobenzene- $^{3,3^{\prime},5,5^{\prime}}$ -tetracarboxylic acid ( $\mathrm{\tilde{{H}}_{4}}$ abtc, $\mathbf{H}_{4}\mathbf{A}3\dot{}$ ) to synthesize three MOFs (SNU-4, SNU- ${\cdot}5^{\prime}$ and SNU-5; SNU represents ‘‘Seoul National University’’) with the same nbo-based topology, and studied the effect of OMSs on $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ adsorption.69 Notably, the formation of OMSs can be controlled precisely by selecting different activation conditions. It was observed that in the presence of OMSs, the MOF has a higher $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ adsorption on both gravimetric and volumetric bases, probably due to a stronger interaction between OMSs with $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ molecules since the reduction of framework mass cannot completely explain the enhanced volumetric uptake.
Combination of $\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}2.5\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ with $^{1,1^{\prime}}$ -azoxybenzene$^{3,3^{\prime},5,5^{\prime}}$ -tetracarboxylate acid $\mathrm{\bf{\tilde{H}}}_{4}\mathrm{\bf{abtc}}$ , $\mathbf{H}_{4}\mathbf{A}\boldsymbol{3}$ ) or trans-stilbene$^{3,3^{\prime},5,5^{\prime}}$ -tetracarboxylic acid $\mathrm{\bf{\tilde{H}}}_{4}\mathrm{\bf{sbtc}}$ , $\mathbf{H}_{4}\mathbf{A}2\dot{\mathbf{\Phi}}$ under solvothermal conditions afforded two isostructural nbo-based MOFs (PCN-10 and PCN-11; PCN represents ‘‘Porous Coordination Network’’).70 The BET surface areas are $1407\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ for PCN-10 and $1931\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ for PCN-11, respectively. Compared to PCN-11, a significant reduction of surface area in PCN-10 is presumably due to the fact that a $\scriptstyle\mathbf{N}=\mathbf{N}$ double bond is thermally less stable than a $\scriptstyle\mathbf{C}=\mathbf{C}$ double bond. This result also suggests that MOFs containing $\scriptstyle\mathbf{C}=\mathbf{C}$ double bonds are more favourable than those with $\scriptstyle\mathbf{N}=\mathbf{N}$ double bonds in retaining permanent porosity after thermal activation. At $77\mathrm{K}$ and 1 atm, PCN-10 adsorbs $2.34\mathrm{wt\%}$ of $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ while PCN-11 adsorbs $2.55\mathrm{wt\%}$ of $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ . At 77 K and 45 bar, the total $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ uptakes are $5.23~\mathrm{wt\%}$ for PCN-10 and $5.97~\mathrm{wt\%}$ for PCN-11. In addition, PCN-11 shows an impressive total $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ uptake of $194~{\mathrm{cm}}^{3}$ (STP) $\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ and 35 bar.
To incorporate a $\mathbf{C}{\equiv}\mathbf{C}$ triple bond into the original organic backbone in MOF-505, Hu et al. synthesized a MOF-505 analogue $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}(\mathrm{ebtc})$ (ebtc $\mathbf{\Psi}=1,1^{\prime}$ -ethynebenzene- $^{\cdot3,3^{\prime},5,5^{\prime}}$ -tetracarboxylate, AT5h)ebayctaivsaotlevdotMheOrFmpalosrseeascstieosnaoBf $\mathbf{H}_{4}$ seubrtfcacweitahr $\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}3\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}.$ $1852\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ and takes up $2.58~\mathrm{wt\%}$ of $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ at $77~\mathrm{K}$ and 1 bar, $178\ \mathrm{cm}^{3}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ and $31\mathrm{cm}^{3}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ of $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ at $273{\mathrm{~K~}}$ and 1 bar. Most significant is that the activated material shows extraordinarily high $\mathbf{C}_{2}\mathbf{H}_{2}$ uptakes of 252 and $160\mathrm{cm}^{3}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ under 1 bar at $273\mathrm{~K~}$ and $295\mathrm{K}_{\sun}$ respectively, which are systematically higher than those of 177 and $148~\mathrm{cm}^{3}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ found for MOF-505.72 Since both frameworks $\mathrm{{(cu_{2}(e b t c)}}$ and MOF-505) have similar OMSs and pore window sites, the arrangement of these open copper sites and their orientation with respect to each other are believed to play a crucial role in such differential interactions with $\mathbf{C}_{2}\mathbf{H}_{2}$ guest molecules. Concretely, the open copper sites deviate from linearity in MOF-505, while the open copper sites are oppositely aligned in $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}(\mathrm{ebtc})$ , which enforces their stronger interactions with the adsorbed $\mathbf{C}_{2}\mathbf{H}_{2}$ molecules. Another possible reason for the higher $\mathbf{C}_{2}\mathbf{H}_{2}$ uptake of $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}(\mathrm{ebtc})$ might be attributed to the $\mathbf{C}{\equiv}\mathbf{C}$ bonds, which enhance their interactions with $\mathbf{C}_{2}\mathbf{H}_{2}$ molecules through weak $\pi{-}\pi$ interactions. The same ligand was also used by other research groups to construct copper-based MOFs. For example, Sun et al.73 synthesized a pair of supramolecular isomers (PCN-16 and PCN-160) by controlling the
assembly of dicopper paddlewheel $\mathrm{{Cu}}_{2}(\mathrm{{COO}})_{4}$ SBUs and ebtc ligands. PCN-16 is virtually the same as $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}(\mathrm{ebtc})$ . Although the two isomers possess the same space group symmetry, their subtle differences in pore structures significantly impact their adsorption capacities. PCN-16 has a BET surface area of $2273~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ and a total pore volume of $1.06~\mathrm{cm}^{3}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , while $\bf{p c N-16^{\prime}}$ possesses a lower BET surface area of $1760\mathrm{~m}^{2}\mathrm{~g}^{-1}$ and a lower pore volume of $0.84~\mathrm{cm}^{3}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ . Accordingly, PCN-16 exhibits significantly higher $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ adsorption capacities than $\bf{p c N-16^{\prime}}$ . PCN-16 and $\bf{p c N-16^{\prime}}$ adsorb 2.6 and $1.7~\mathrm{wt\%}$ of $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ at $77\mathrm{K}$ and 1 atm, and 191 and $111{\mathrm{cm}}^{3}\left({\mathrm{STP}}\right){\mathrm{cm}}^{-3}$ of total $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ at $300~\mathrm{K}$ and 35 bar, respectively.
An nbo-based MOF, PCN-46, was constructed by a solvothermal reaction of 5-ethynylisophthalic acid and $\operatorname{Cu}(\operatorname{OAc})_{2}$ . Interestingly, the one-pot self-assembly process involves three-step sequential reactions: the oxidative coupling of 5-ethynylisophthalic acid followed by deprotonation of the di-isophthalic acid and the formation of MOF. PCN-46 exhibits impressive gas sorption capacities. It adsorbs $1.95~\mathrm{wt\%}$ of $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ at $77\mathrm{~K~}$ and 1 atm, rising to $7.39\ \mathrm{wt\%}$ at $77~\mathrm{K}$ and 97 bar. The isosteric enthalpy of $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ adsorption of $7.2\mathrm{\kJ\mol^{-1}}$ at a low coverage is higher than those of other nbo-based MOFs such as NOTT-101 $(5.3\mathrm{kJ}\mathrm{mol}^{-1})$ , and NOTT-102 $(5.4\mathrm{\kJ\mol^{-1}})$ , which might be attributed to the interaction between $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ molecules and exposed and delocalized $\pi$ electrons in the polyyne unit in the ligand. In fact, a strong interaction between $\mathbf{C}_{2}\mathbf{H}_{2}$ and an nbo-based MOF $\mathrm{{Cu}}_{2}(\mathrm{ebtc})$ containing alkyne units was also discovered.71 In addition, a comparison of PCN-46 with NOTT-101 and NOTT-102 indicates that replacement of phenyl rings by polyyne units leads to a boost of pore volume and $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ uptake. The $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ sorption properties were also investigated. The total $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ uptake is $172~{\mathrm{cm}}^{3}$ (STP) $\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ and 35 bar. The total $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ uptake is $22.5\mathrm{\mmol\g}^{-1}$ at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ and 30 bar. Zhang et al.74 also made the same MOF, which was directly obtained by a solvothermal reaction of $^{1,1^{\prime}}$ -butadiynebenzene- $^{3,3^{\prime},5,5^{\prime}}$ -tetracarboxylate (bbtc, A6) and $\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}3\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ . Compared to PCN-46, the resulting MOF $\mathrm{{Cu}}_{2}(\mathsf{b}\mathsf{b}\mathrm{{tc})}$ exhibits a significantly lower BET surface area (1014 and $2500~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ for $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}(\mathsf{b}\mathsf{b}\mathrm{tc})$ and PCN-46, respectively) and $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ uptake $(1.43~\mathrm{wt\%}$ and $1.95~\mathrm{wt\%}$ at $77~\mathrm{K}$ and 1 bar for $\mathrm{{Cu}}_{2}(\mathsf{b}\mathsf{b}\mathrm{tc})$ and PCN-46, respectively), which may be due to the different activation profiles and/or the quality of the samples.
By utilizing a long tetracarboxylate with imide groups, $N,N^{\prime}$ -bis(3,5-dicarboxyphenyl)pyromellitic diimide $\left(\mathrm{H}_{4}\mathrm{A}24\right)$ , Suh et al.75 synthesized two MOFs SNU-50 and SNU-51 by solvothermal reactions of the ligand with $\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}2.5\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and $\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ respectively. The two MOFs have entirely different structures. In SNU-50, the ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ ions form dicopper paddlewheel $\mathrm{{Cu}_{2}(\mathrm{{COO})_{4}}}$ clusters serving as square-planar SBUs that are linked with the rectangular organic building blocks to give rise to a 3D nbobased network, while in SNU-51, the rectangular tetracarboxylates are linked with the distorted tetrahedral $\mathrm{Zn_{2}(C O O)_{4}}$ SBUs to form a pts-based network. Interestingly, the $\mathrm{Zn(n)}$ ions in SNU-51 can be exchanged with $\mathrm{{Cu}(\Omega)}$ ions with the retention of the pts-based net that is impossible to be produced through direct solvothermal synthesis. After desolvation, SNU-50 retained its structural integrity while SNU-51 collapsed. The desolvated SNU-50 exhibits a BET surface area of $2300\mathrm{~m}^{2}\mathrm{~g}^{-1}$ , and high adsorption capacities for $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ , $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ , and $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ . It adsorbs $7.85\mathrm{wt\%}$ of $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ at 77 K and 60 bar, $17.5\mathrm{mmol}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ at $298\mathrm{K}$ and 55 bar, and $155\mathrm{cm}^{3}\left(\mathrm{STP}\right)\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ of $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ at $298\mathrm{K}$ and 60 bar. The high adsorption capacities of SNU50 might be attributed to its unique structural features combining the large pore volume, exposed copper sites, and imide functional groups. For comparison, $\mathbf{NOTI-103}^{67}$ constructed from $\mathrm{{Cu}(\Omega_{\mathrm{{I}}})}$ ions and a long tetracarboxylate (A16) exhibits a lower total $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ uptake capacity of $7.22\mathrm{wt\%}$ at 77 K and 60 bar despite a higher BET surface area of $2930\ \mathrm{m}^{2}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , indicating that a MOF with imide groups might be much better for $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ uptake than a MOF containing simple benzene rings.
Some experimental and computational studies show that bridging ligands with an aromatic-rich backbone can enhance the gas–framework interactions via the polarized $\pi$ electrons. Thus, an aromatic-rich tetracarboxylate, $(R)\mathbf{-}6,6^{\prime}$ -dichloro- $^{}\cdot2,2^{\prime}\cdot$ diethoxy- $^{\cdot1,1^{\prime}}$ -binaphthyl- $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -di(5-isophthalic acid) $\left(\mathrm{H}_{4}\mathrm{A}22\right)$ was designed and synthesized by our research group, and the corresponding nbo-based MOF UTSA- $\mathbf{40}^{76}$ (UTSA represents ‘‘University of Texas at San Antonio’’) was obtained by a solvothermal reaction of the ligand and $\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}2.5\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ in $\mathrm{DMF/EtOH/H_{2}O}$ at $363\mathrm{~K~}$ under acidic conditions for $^{48\mathrm{~h~}}$ . Due to the presence of the chiral binaphthyl groups in the organic linkers, UTSA-40 crystallizes in the chiral space group $R32$ . After desolvation, UTSA-40a shows a moderately high surface area of $1630~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ and a pore volume of $0.65~\mathrm{cm}^{3}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ . These values are comparable to those of MOF-505 but lower than those of NOTT-102. High-pressure gas sorption properties were studied in detail. $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ adsorption capacities are much higher than the corresponding value for $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ , indicating UTSA-40a has the potential for use in $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ purification processes, which is comprehensively examined by IAST (Ideal Adsorbed Solution Theory) and breakthrough calculations. It was found that UTSA40a possesses higher working capacity and lower regeneration cost when operating at high pressure than the traditionally used zeolites NaX and LTA-5A. Furthermore, compared with the well examined MOFs $\mathbf{MgMOF-74}^{77}$ and Cu-TDPAT78 the regeneration cost of UTSA-40a is significantly lower despite its somewhat lower productivity.
In general, significant enhancement of the surface area could be achieved by ligand extension. Following this strategy, Bai et al.79 constructed a new MOF-505 analogue, NJU-Bai12 (NJU-Bai represents ‘‘Nanjing University Bai’s group’’), with a high BET surface area of $3038~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , which was assembled from dicopper paddlewheel $\mathrm{{Cu}}_{2}(\mathrm{{COO})_{4}}$ SBUs and a nanosized tetracarboxylate, $^{5,5^{\prime}}$ -(1,4-phenylene-2,1-ethynediyl)bis(1,3-benzene)- dicarboxylic acid, ( $\mathrm{\tilde{{H}}}_{4}$ pdeb, $\mathbf{H}_{4}\mathbf{A}29\dot{}$ ). As expected, the ligand extension enlarges the sizes of the two polyhedral cages, their diameters reaching up to ca. 1.1 and $1.6~\mathrm{{nm}}$ , respectively. Gas adsorption properties were investigated accordingly, and the results show that the evacuated sample NJU-Bai12 takes up $6.27~\mathrm{wt\%}$ of $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ at $77\mathrm{~K~}$ and 20 bar, which is among the highest of the MOF-505 series reported so far under the same conditions (the current highest one is $6.51\mathrm{wt\%}$ in NOTT-103a).67 The initial isosteric heat of $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ adsorption of $6.8~\mathrm{kJ~mol^{-1}}$ is comparable to that of PCN-46 $(7.2\mathrm{kJ}\mathrm{mol}^{-1})_{-}$ , but higher than those of NOTT-101 and NOTT-102 (6.3 and $5.3\mathrm{kJ}\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ for NOTT-101 and NOTT-102, respectively), which is presumably due to the stronger interaction between $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ molecules and the delocalized $\pi$ electrons in the alkyne units than that for the phenyl rings. In addition, NJU-Bai12 also exhibits an excellent $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ uptake capacity of $19.85\mathrm{~mmol~g^{-1}}$ at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ and 20 bar, as well as selective gas adsorption properties with a $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ selectivity of 5.0 and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ selectivity of 24.6 at $298\ \mathrm{K}.$ . Later, a similar MOF compound HNUST-2 (HNUST represents ‘‘Hunan University of Science and Technology’’) was prepared in which the tetracarboxylate ligand was simply replaced by $^{5,5^{\prime}}$ -naphthalene-1,4- diylbis(ethyne-2,1-diyl)diisophthalic acid $\mathrm{\cdot}\mathrm{\Pi}_{\mathrm{{H}}_{4}\mathrm{{nded}}}$ , $\mathbf{H_{4}A30}\mathbf{\dot{}}$ ).80 HNUST-2 exhibits a high BET surface area of 2366 m2 g1, and impressive adsorption capacities for $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ ( $18.07~\mathrm{mmol}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ and 20 bar), $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ $85.6~\mathrm{cm}^{3}$ (STP) $\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ and 20 bar), and $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ $(5.2~\mathrm{wt\%}$ at $77\mathrm{~K~}$ and 20 bar), which are systematically lower than those of NJU-Bai12.
Using bis(3,5-dicarboxyphenyl)terephthalamide $\left(\mathrm{H}_{4}\mathrm{A}23\right)$ containing four carboxylate groups as coordination sites and two amide groups as functional sites, a MOF-505 analogue HNUST-1 was constructed by Zheng et al.81 After activation, HNUST-1 exhibits a moderate BET surface area of $1620~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ and pore volume of $0.571~\mathrm{cm}^{3}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , which are lower than those predicted from the single-crystal X-ray structure. The relatively lower surface area indicates that the framework undergoes partial collapse after activation. Despite this, HNUST-1 still exhibits a high $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ uptake capacity of $93~\mathrm{cm}^{3}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ and 1 bar, as well as good $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ selectivities of 7.2 and 39.8 at $273\mathrm{~K~}$ respectively. Furthermore, the adsorption enthalpies of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ at zero coverage are calculated to be 31.2 and $23.4\mathrm{kJ}\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ respectively. The higher $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption enthalpy in HNUST-1 is attributed to the large quadrupolar moment of the $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ molecule facilitating strong dipole–quadrupole interactions between the acylamide groups and $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ . This work highlights the fact that the strategy of inserting the bridging amide groups into MOF frameworks is a promising way to construct new porous materials for effective enhancement of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ capture capacity and selectivity. Along this strategy, a microporous nbo-based MOF HNUST-3 containing oxalamide units was synthesized by the self-assembly of dicopper paddlewheel $\mathrm{{Cu}_{2}(\mathrm{{COO})_{4}}}$ SBUs and a tetracarboxylate ligand containing functional oxalamide groups, $\boldsymbol{N},\boldsymbol{N}^{\prime}$ -bis(3,5- dicarboxylatephenyl)oxalamide $\mathrm{\(H_{4}A7)}$ .82 The presence of open copper sites and polar oxalamide groups in the framework leads to high adsorption selectivity of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ toward $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and ${\bf N}_{2}$ . The Henry selectivites of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ reach up to 7.9 and 26.1 at $298\mathrm{K}$ , respectively. In addition, the high pressure gas sorption properties were examined. HNUST-3 can adsorb a total $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ of $6.1~\mathrm{wt\%}$ at $77\mathrm{K}$ and 20 bar, excess $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ of $20.23~\mathrm{mmol}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ and 20 bar, and excess $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ of $135.8~\mathrm{cm}^{3}$ (STP) $\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ and 20 bar.
The incorporation of accessible nitrogen donor sites into the pore walls of porous materials can drastically impact their gas uptake capacity and selectivity, especially for the $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ capture due to the dipole–quadrupole interactions between the polarizable $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ molecule and the accessible nitrogen site. Thus, the introduction of a nitrogen-rich 1,2,3-triazole ring into MOFs will be expected to improve the performance of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption. Based on this idea, a novel 1,2,3-triazole-containing tetratopic ligand, $^{5,5^{\prime}}$ -(1H-1,2,3-triazole-1,4-diyl)-diisophthalic acid $\mathbf{(H_{4}A41)}$ , was designed and synthesized via a copper-catalyzed click reaction. A solvothermal reaction of $\mathbf{H_{4}A41}$ with $\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ in DMF afforded NTU-101-Zn (NTU represents ‘‘Nanyang Technological University’’).83 In the structure, each tetrahedral $\mathrm{Zn_{2}(C O O)_{4}}$ SBU is linked with square-planar tetracarboxylate, generating a 3D pts-based network where the coordination-free triazole rings are exposed inside the channels. The activated NTU-101-Zn shows a very poor gas sorption capacity, which is expected due to the framework collapse upon activation. However, when the $\mathrm{Zn(n)}$ ions in the framework were post-synthetically exchanged with $\operatorname{Cu}(\operatorname{II})$ ions in a single-crystal to single-crystal (SCSC) fashion, the resulting NTU-101-Cu exhibited significantly improved gas adsorption properties. The activated NTU-101-Cu exhibits a BET surface area of $2017\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , and takes up $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ of $1.78~\mathrm{wt\%}$ at $77\mathrm{K}$ and 1 atm, $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ of $101\ \mathrm{cm}^{3}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ and $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ of $20~\mathrm{cm}^{3}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at $273\mathrm{~K~}$ and 1 atm. The high adsorption selectivity towards $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ over $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ is attributed to the open copper sites and the free nitrogen donor sites which are accessible within the porous frameworks for efficient interactions with $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ molecules.
To systematically study the effects of various functional groups on gas sorption properties of MOFs, four isostructural MOFs (PCN-305 to PCN-308) with various functionalized pore surfaces were synthesized from a series of V-shaped di-isophthalate ligands $\left(\mathrm{H}_{4}\mathrm{A}37–\mathrm{H}_{4}\mathrm{A}40\right)$ .84 Solvothermal reaction of $^{5,5^{\prime}}$ -(pyridine-3,5-diyl)diisophthalic acid ( $\mathrm{\tilde{~H}}_{4}\mathrm{pdda}$ , $\mathbf{H_{4}A37}$ ) and $\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}2.5\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ in DMA with a small amount of $\mathrm{HBF_{4}}$ afforded green crystals of PCN-305. Attempts to obtain the single crystals of other MOF compounds (PCN-306 to PCN-308) failed, but PXRD studies confirmed that they are isostructural with PCN305. In PCN-305, there are 1D irregular channels along the $c$ axis, and the uncoordinated pyridyl functional groups point to the pore of the framework. Their gas sorption isotherms were examined. It was found that there is no apparent relationship between the gas sorption properties and different functional groups, and gas sorption is basically related to the textural properties in these isostructural MOFs. Of these compounds, PCN-306 without any functional group has the highest surface area and pore volume. PCN-305 with pyridyl functional groups has the highest selectivity of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ over ${\bf N}_{2}$ , whereas PCN307 with methyl groups has the highest selectivity of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ over $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ . PCN-308 with $\mathrm{CF}_{3}$ functional groups exhibits a slightly higher $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ uptake of $2.67~\mathrm{wt\%}$ at $77~\mathrm{K}$ and 1 atm. Note that the same MOF compound with pyridyl functional groups was also independently reported by Bai et al., and named as NJUBai10.85 The high-pressure gas sorption properties were explored in detail. The desolvated NJU-Bai10 exhibits the saturated excess volumetric $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ uptake of $48\ \mathrm{g}\ \mathrm{L}^{-1}$ at $77~\mathrm{K}$ and 60 bar, which is the highest value ever reported among MOF materials in terms of the saturated excess volumetric uptake. The total $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ uptake capacities reach $9.5~\mathrm{wt\%}$ $(70.1\ \mathrm{g}\ \mathrm{L}^{-1})$ at $77\mathrm{K}$ and 100 bar, and $198.6\mathrm{cm}^{3}\left(\mathrm{STP}\right)\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ at $290\mathrm{K}$ and 35 bar, respectively. The saturated $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ uptake is $20.5\mathrm{mmol}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ and 40 bar.
There has been an escalating interest in constructing metalloporphyrin based MOF materials due to their potential applications for gas storage, sensors, and particularly heterogeneous catalysis.86 More recently, Ma et al. reported the synthesis of a porphyrin based MOF in which [5,15-bis(dicarboxyphenyl)- porphyrin]copper(II) (Cuddcpp, Cu-A27) was linked by dicopper paddlewheel $\mathrm{{Cu}_{2}(\mathrm{{COO})_{4}}}$ SBUs to form MMPF-1 (MMPF represents ‘‘Metal–MetalloPorphyrin Framework’’).87 In the assembly process, insertion of a ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ ion into the center of the $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{ddcpp}$ unit (A27) occurred in situ. The structure of MMPF-1 consists of rhombicuboctahedra cages serving as supramolecular building blocks (SBBs) to sustain the 3D porous metalloporphyrin framework structure. In each cage, there are 8 open copper sites associated with the porphyrin rings of the ligands and 8 open copper sites from dicopper paddlewheel $\mathrm{{Cu}_{2}(\mathrm{{COO})_{4}}}$ SBUs (Fig. 2a). The ABAB packing of the polyhedral cages in MMPF1 significantly constricts its pore size (Fig. 2b), which facilitates selective adsorption of $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ and $\mathbf{O}_{2}$ over ${\bf N}_{2}$ , and $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ over $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ . At $77\mathrm{~K~}$ and 1 atm, MMPF-1 takes up a large amount of $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ $(50\ \mathrm{cm}^{3}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1})$ but a very limited amount of $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ $(5\ \mathrm{cm}^{3}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1})$ (Fig. 2c). At $196\mathrm{~K~}$ and 1 atm, MMPF-1 takes up a large amount of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ $(80\mathrm{cm}^{3}\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ , which is much higher than the amount of $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ $(18\ \mathrm{cm}^{3}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1})$ (Fig. 2d).
Typically, it is necessary to have rigid organic linkers and SBUs to construct stable porous frameworks, as discussed above; however, recent studies show that some semirigid and even flexible organic linkers can also be used to synthesize robust MOFs. For example, Zhou et al. reported two $\mathrm{{Cu}_{2}(\mathrm{{COO})_{4}}}$ -based MOFs (PCN-12 and $\mathbf{PCN-1}2^{\prime}.$ ) using a semirigid ligand, $^{5,5^{\prime}}$ - methylene-di-isophthalate $\mathrm{\cdotH_{4}m d i p}$ , $\mathbf{H}_{4}\mathbf{A}32$ ).88 Their structures are quite different from those of the MOFs assembled from linear di-isophthalates. In PCN-12, 12 dicopper paddlewheel units are linked with 24 isophthalates to form a cuboctahedral cage connecting to six others in three orthogonal directions to form a 3D net (Fig. 3a), while in $\mathbf{p}\mathbf{CN-1}2^{\prime}$ , 6 dicopper paddlewheel units connect with 3 ligands to form a trigonal prism connecting to six others to form a 3D net (Fig. 3b). Notably, after desolvation, 12 open copper sites in cuboctahedral cages in PCN12 point toward the center of the cage, while all the open metal coordination sites in $\mathbf{PCN-1}2^{\prime}$ point away from the cavity of the polyhedron. Such different OMS alignment results in a remarkable difference in $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ uptakes for the two MOFs. PCN-12 exhibits the current second record-high $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ uptake of $3.05~\mathrm{wt\%}$ at $77\mathrm{~K~}$ and 1 atm, in contrast to an uptake of $2.4\mathrm{wt\%}$ for $\mathbf{p}\mathbf{CN-1}2^{\prime}$ under the same conditions (Fig. 3c).
Liang et al.89 used a semirigid tetracarboxylate ligand, 5-(3,5- dicarboxybenzyloxy)isophthalic acid $(\mathrm{H_{4}d b i p,\ H_{4}A4})_{:}$ to construct a robust copper-based compound with the same nbo based topology as MOF-505. After the removal of the guest and coordinated water molecules, the resulting material exhibits a high BET surface area of $1773\mathrm{~m}^{2}\mathrm{~g}^{-1}$ and exceptionally high $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption of $173\ \mathrm{cm}^{3}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at $273{\mathrm{~K~}}$ and 0.95 bar, as well as good $\mathbf{CO}_{2}/\mathbf{N}_{2}$ selectivity at $273\mathrm{~K~}$ , which might be attributed to the structural features such as a cage-like framework and open copper sites. Presumably due to the semirigid nature of the ligand, combination of the same ligand with dicopper paddlewheel $\mathrm{{Cu}_{2}(\mathrm{{COO})_{4}}}$ SBUs under different solvothermal conditions resulted in another compound with distinctly different architecture, which was reported by Goldberg et al.90 The dehydrated sample, however, exhibits a relatively low BET surface area of only $232~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$
Subsequent to the publication reporting PCN-12, a series of MOFs (UHM-2, UHM-3, and UHM-4; UHM represents ‘‘University of Hamburg Materials’’), which are isoreticular to PCN-12, were synthesized to systematically investigate the influence of the incorporation of atoms with higher polarizability on the $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ uptakes.91,92 They comprise dicopper paddlewheel $[\mathrm{Cu}_{2}(\mathrm{COO})_{4}]$ units as connectors, and $^{5,5^{\prime}}$ -(propane-2,2-diyl)diisophthalate (dmcdip, $\mathbf{H_{4}A}33$ ), $^{5,5^{\prime}}$ -(dimethylsilanediyl)diisophthalate (dmsdip, $\mathrm{H}_{4}\mathrm{A}34)$ and $^{5,5^{\prime}}$ -(dimethylgermanediyl)diisophthalate (dmgdip, $\mathbf{H_{4}A35}$ ), respectively, as linkers. After the optimal thermal activation, the materials exhibit the specific surface area of 1692, 2430, and $1360\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , respectively. Furthermore, it was found that the $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ uptakes at $77\mathrm{~K~}$ and 1 bar as well as the isoreticular heat of adsorption values slightly increase in the order of $\mathbf{X}=\mathbf{\partial}$ $\mathbf{C}<\mathbf{Si}<\mathbf{Ge}$ . However, due to the incomplete solvent removal during the thermal treatment, it is difficult to derive a positive correlation between the polarizability of linker atoms and the $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ uptake. Indeed, DFT calculations reveal that the effect is relatively marginal with respect to the effect of geometric changes associated with the interchange of the central atom.
When the linker dmsdip incorporated in the MOF UHM-3 was extended by two phenyl rings, the resulting ligand $\mathrm{H_{4}A44}$ , when combined with dicopper paddlewheel units, afforded a topologically different network, called UHM-6, with two-fold interpenetration,93 which is isoreticular to that of PMOF-3 (PMOF represents ‘‘polyhedron based MOF’’) constructed from the ligand 1,3-bis(3,5-dicarboxylphenylethynyl)benzene $\mathbf{\left(H_{4}A45\right)}$ .94 In the single net, there are three different cages. The first cage corresponds to one cuboctahedron consisting of 24 bdc units of the ligands and 12 dicopper paddlewheel units. Each cuboctahedron is connected to the neighboring cuboctahedra by the linkers assuming the $C_{\mathrm{s}}$ conformation along the $a\mathrm{-}$ and $b$ -axes, and the ones with $C_{2}$ symmetry along the $c$ axis. Such different types of linkage generated two other cages. One is surrounded by 8 paddlewheels and 4 linker molecules having $C_{2}$ symmetry, and the other one is bordered by 2 paddlewheels that are connected by 4 linkers with $C_{\mathrm{s}}$ symmetry. The gas sorption was investigated accordingly. In comparison to PMOF-3, UHM-6 possesses a notably lower specific surface area (1200 vs. $1840\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ but has only a slightly lower $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ uptake (1.8 vs. $2.1~\mathrm{wt\%}$ at $77~\mathrm{K}$ and 1 bar).
In the following section, we will focus on some promising MOF materials for high-pressure $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ storage, which are constructed from the dicopper paddlewheel $\mathrm{{Cu}_{2}(\mathrm{{COO})_{4}}}$ SBUs and di-isophthalate ligands.
A microporous material PCN-14 was synthesized by the selfassembly of $^{5,5^{\prime}}$ -(9,10-anthracediyl)diisophthalate (adip, A15) and dicopper paddlewheel $\mathrm{[Cu_{2}(C O O)_{4}]}$ SBUs.95 The framework has two remarkable structural features: (1) every 12 adip ligands connect 6 dicopper paddlewheel SBUs to form a squashed cuboctahedral cage (Fig. 4a), bridging the anthracenyl rings in close proximity $(2.6\mathrm{~\AA~}$ between a H atom and the center of a phenyl ring from the adjacent anthracenyl group). Such an orientation of organic linkers within the framework system leads to the enhanced van der Waals potential pocket where $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ can interact strongly with the frameworks; (2) the removal of coordinated water molecules from the terminal sites of the dicopper paddlewheel SBUs results in the formation of open copper sites that are approximately oriented toward the center of the cuboctahedral cages so as to maximize electrostatic interactions with guest species. The combination of a small pore diameter and available open copper sites permits a high absolute volumetric $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ uptake of $230\ \mathrm{cm}^{3}\left(\mathrm{STP}\right)\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ at $290\mathrm{K}$ and 35 bar (Fig. 4b), which is higher than the widely referred DOE (Department of Energy) target of $180~\mathrm{cm}^{3}$ (STP) $\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ for $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ storage. The high $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ adsorption capacity of PCN-14 was also confirmed by the recent studies (Fig. 4b).96,97
Snurr et al.98 employed a high-throughput computational screening method to identify a known MOF NOTT-107 with a high $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ uptake, and predicted a potential storage capacity of $213~\mathrm{cm}^{3}$ (STP) $\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ and 35 bar. The reason for such a high $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ uptake in NOTT-107 is believed to be similar to the one behind PCN-14.
A series of MOFs (NOTT-100, NOTT-101, NOTT-102, NOTT-103, NOTT-109) have been chosen and examined as potential adsorbents for $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ storage.99 They exhibit high $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ adsorption capacities at $300~\mathrm{K}$ and 35 bar $\mathrm{181-196~cm^{3}}$ (STP) $\mathrm{cm}^{-3},$ and excellent working capacities for $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ at $300\mathrm{~K~}$ ( $\mathrm{104-140~cm^{3}}$ (STP) $\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ , from 35 to 5 bar). The gravimetric $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ uptake in this MOF series systematically increases with increasing porosity, while their $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ storage pore occupancy decreases with increasing pore size. Most significantly, an empirical equation was derived from the high-pressure $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ adsorption isotherms capable of predicting reasonably well the $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ storage performance of previously reported microporous MOF materials with a pore volume of less than $1.50~\mathrm{cm}^{3}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , thus providing a convenient method to screen MOFs for $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ storage purposes.
A new tetracarboxylic acid, $^{5,5^{\prime}}$ -(9H-fluorene-2,7-diyl)diisophthalic acid ( $\mathrm{\tilde{H}}_{4}$ fddi, $\mathbf{H_{4}A17}$ ), was developed by Duan et al.100 Reaction of this ligand with $\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}2.5\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ under solvothermal conditions afforded a microporous MOF named as ZJU-25 (ZJU represents ‘‘Zhejiang University’’), displaying a new topology derived from ssb instead of nbo. After activation, ZJU-25 exhibits a BET surface area of $2124~\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , higher than that of MOF-505 but lower than that of NOTT-102. More importantly, the open copper sites $\left(1.21\mathrm{nm}^{-3}\right)$ ) and suitable pore spaces $\left(5{-}9\mathring{\mathbf{A}}\right)$ within ZJU-25a have enabled this MOF to take up a large amount of $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ . At $300~\mathrm{K}$ and 35 bar, the absolute uptake of $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ reaches the widely referred DOE target of $180~\mathrm{cm}^{3}$ (STP) $\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ . The $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ storage can be further increased to $227\mathrm{cm}^{3}\left(\mathrm{STP}\right)\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ at $300\mathrm{K}$ and 63 bar. Shortly afterward, the same group reported another porous MOF ZJU-5 constructed using a new tetracarboxylate with Lewis basic pyridyl sites, $^{5,5^{\prime}}$ -(pyridine-2,5-diyl)diisophthalic acid $\mathrm{\big[H_{4}p d d i}$ , $\mathbf{H}_{4}\mathbf{A}13\mathbf{\dot{\Omega}}_{.}$ ).101 Intriguingly, the interplay between moderately high volumetric BET surface area $(1916~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{cm}^{-3})$ , open copper sites $\left(1.54~\mathrm{nm}^{-3}\right)$ , and Lewis basic pyridyl sites $\left(0.77{\mathrm{~nm}}^{-3}\right)$ has enabled ZJU-5a to take up a large amount of $\mathbf{C}_{2}\mathbf{H}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ . At $273\mathrm{~K~}$ and 1 atm, the $\mathbf{C}_{2}\mathbf{H}_{2}$ storage capacity reaches $290{\mathrm{~cm}}^{3}{\mathrm{~g}}^{-1}$ , which is the highest ever reported among any porous materials.34 At $298\mathrm{~K~}$ and 1 atm, the $\mathbf{C}_{2}\mathbf{H}_{2}$ uptake of $193\mathrm{cm}^{3}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ is only slightly lower than $201\mathrm{cm}^{3}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ and $197\mathrm{cm}^{3}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ in two best MOFs (HKUST-1 and CoMOF-74) for C2H2 storage.72,102 The $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ storage capacity reaches $190~\mathrm{cm}^{3}\left(\mathrm{STP}\right)\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ at $300~\mathrm{K}$ and 35 bar, which can be further increased to $224~\mathrm{{cm}}^{3}$ (STP) $\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ at $300\mathrm{~K~}$ and 60 bar, about $85\%$ of the new DOE target of $263\ \mathrm{\cm}^{3}$ (STP) $\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ for material based $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ storage if the packing loss is ignored.103
A carborane-based MOF NU-135 (NU represents ‘‘Northwestern University’’) with the same nbo based topology as that of the archetypal MOF-505 was constructed via a solvothermal reaction between $\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}2.5\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and a boron-rich ligand $\left(\mathrm{H}_{4}\mathrm{A}26\right)$ consisting of a central quasi-spherical para-carborane unit and two terminal isophthalic acid groups.104 Compared to an isoreticular MOF NOTT-101 that contains a phenylene group in the middle of two isophthalic acid groups, which is approximately equal in length to a $p$ -carborane, NU-135 has a slightly reduced pore volume (1.02 and $1.08~\mathrm{cm}^{3}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ for NU-135 and NOTT-101, respectively)
but a greatly enhanced volumetric surface area (1900 and $1460~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ for NU-135 and NOTT-101, respectively) as a consequence of the globular $p$ -carborane subunit. Significantly, the interplay between the volumetric surface area and pore volume results in high volumetric adsorption in NU-135. The total volumetric $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ storage capacity of NU-135 at $298~\mathrm{~K~}$ reaches up to $\mathrm{187~cm^{3}}$ (STP) $\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ and $230~\mathrm{cm}^{3}$ (STP) $\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ at 35 bar and 65 bar, respectively. The $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ working capacity from 65 to 5 bar at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ is $170\mathrm{cm}^{3}$ (STP) $\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ . The total volumetric $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ storage capacity at $77~\mathrm{K}$ and 55 bar is $49\ \mathrm{g}\ \mathrm{L}^{-1}$ . The $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ uptake at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ and 30 bar is $18.5~\mathrm{mmol}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ . These properties are comparable to those of current record holders in the area of CH4 and H2 storage.32,33
2.1.2 Other metal di-isophthalate frameworks. Because of the strong binding of free ${\mathrm{Li}}^{+}$ sites with $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ molecules, loading ${\mathrm{Li}}^{+}$ ions into MOF structures is expected to be a promising approach to significantly enhance the $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ –host interaction. Schro¨der et al.105–108 studied a series of anionic $\operatorname{In}(\operatorname{In}).$ –tetracarboxylate frameworks constructed from functionalized diisophthalate linkers of different lengths (Scheme 2). The organic cations within the as-synthesized materials can be exchanged with ${\mathrm{Li}}^{+}$ ions to afford the corresponding $\mathrm{Li}^{+}.$ -exchanged frameworks. Based on the coordination geometry at the indium centers and bridging ligands, these framework structures can be divided into the following two groups: (I) NOTT-204, NOTT205, NOTT-210, NOTT-211, NOTT-212 and NOTT-213; and (II) NOTT-200, NOTT-201, NOTT-206, NOTT-207, NOTT-208 and NOTT-209. The materials in the first group (I) crystallize in a tetragonal space group. Each $\operatorname{In}\left(\operatorname{III}\right)$ center adopts an 8-coordinated geometry via binding to $^{\mathrm{~\small~O~}}$ -centers from four carboxylate groups to give a tetrahedral 4-connected metal node. Each ligand binds to four separate $\operatorname{In}(\operatorname{In})$ centers and acts as a square planar 4-connected node. Thus, these frameworks
## NOTT-205
cheme 2 Structures of the organic ligands $H_{4}L$ and resulting MOF materials.
have a non-interpenetrated pts-based structure. In the second group (II), the framework structures crystallize in a monoclinic space group and consist of two independent doubly interpenetrated networks. Each $\operatorname{In}\left(\operatorname{InI}\right)$ center in the parent framework is 7-coordinated via binding to $^{\circ}$ -centers from three bidentate chelating carboxylate groups and one monodentate carboxylate group, while in ${\mathrm{\Li}}^{+}$ -exchanged samples, $\mathrm{In\big(m\big)}$ centers are 8-coordinated by 8 oxygen atoms from four bidentate chelating carboxylate groups. Each ligand binds to 4 separate $\mathrm{In\big(m\big)}$ centers to yield an overall 4-connected diamond type structure for the anionic frameworks.
As shown in Fig. 5a, these anionic frameworks are triporous. When going from the as-synthesized samples to $\mathrm{Li}^{+}$ -exchanged samples, the cations in channel B are replaced by ${\mathrm{Li}}^{+}$ cations located within channel C. In the first group, replacing the organic cations $\mathrm{Me}_{2}\mathrm{NH}_{2}^{+}$ in NOTT-204 by ${\bf L i}^{+}$ produces a more porous material NOTT-205 with higher $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ adsorption capacity. The increase in $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ capacity for NOTT-205 compared to NOTT204 mainly arises from an enlargement in pore volume rather than any increase in $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ adsorption enthalpy. The other members within the group (I), however, are not stable and lose porosity. In the second group, the ${\bf N}_{2}$ isotherms of the desolvated NOTT-206, NOTT200 and NOTT-208 show no adsorption, hysteretic desorption and fully reversible sorption, respectively (Fig. 5b). After $\mathrm{Li}^{+}$ exchange, the resulting desolvated NOTT-207, NOTT-201, and NOTT-209 show type-I isotherms with good reversibility (Fig. 5c). Similar kinetic trap effects were also observed in the $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ sorption isotherms at $77\mathrm{K}.$ The $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ desorption isotherms for NOTT-206 and NOTT-200 do not track the adsorption isotherms and therefore show marked hysteresis loops (Fig. 5d). Upon $\operatorname{Li}^{+}.$ -exchange, the $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ adsorption isotherms for the desolvated NOTT-207 and NOTT-201 show no hysteresis but enhanced $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ adsorption properties in terms of both capacities and adsorption enthalpies (Fig. 5e). In contrast, both desolvated NOTT208 and NOTT-209 having larger pores show reversible $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ sorption isotherms without hysteresis. An enhancement of $31\%$ in the $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ storage capacity together with an increase of $38\%$ in the isosteric heat of $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ adsorption is observed on going from NOTT-208 to NOTT-209. Considering that NOTT-208 and NOTT-209 have similar ${\bf N}_{2}$ uptake capacities and BET surface areas, the enhancement of $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ storage capacities mainly originates from a change in the charge distribution in the $[\mathrm{In}(\mathrm{O}_{2}\mathrm{CR})_{4}]$ moiety by incorporation of $\mathrm{Li}^{+}$ ions, because inelastic neutron scattering (INS) experiments have confirmed that the adsorbed $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ molecules are predominantly interacting with the carboxylate groups rather than directly with the ${\bf L i}^{+}$ ions.
Solvothermal reaction of ${\mathrm{Sc}}(\mathbf{OSO}_{2}\mathbf{CF}_{3})_{3}{\cdot}x\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ with $\mathbf{H}_{4}$ bptc $\mathbf{\Pi}(\mathbf{H}_{4}\mathbf{A}1)$ in a slightly acidified mixture of DMF–THF– $\mathbf{\cdotH}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ afforded the framework NOTT-400.109 Each $\operatorname{Sc}(\operatorname{III})$ center was bridged by carboxylate groups to form 1D chains, which are further linked to each other through tetracarboxylate anions to give rise to an overall 3D framework structure where 1D channels of an approximate size of 8.1 Å were formed along the $b$ axis. Desolvated NOTT-400a shows a high BET surface area of $1350\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ among the Sc(III) coordination polymers, and as a consequence, achieves the highest $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ uptake for a $\operatorname{Sc}(\operatorname{III})$ based MOF material. The $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ uptake for NOTT-400a at $77\mathrm{~K~}$ and 1.0 bar is $2.14~\mathrm{wt\%}$ , rising to $3.84~\mathrm{wt\%}$ at 20 bar. The isosteric heat of $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ adsorption was estimated to be $5.96\ \mathrm{kJ\mol^{-1}}$ at zero coverage.
Feng et al.110 constructed a porous Mg-based MOF, CPF-1 (CPF represents ‘‘crystalline porous materials’’), which was obtained by a solvothermal reaction of $\mathrm{{H_{4}b p t c}}$ $\mathrm{(H}_{4}\mathrm{A}1)$ and $\mathbf{Mg(NO_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6H_{2}O}$ in a mixed solvent containing $N\cdot$ -ethylformamide (NEF). The formation of CPF-1 is highly sensitive to the type of solvents being used. Only $\mathrm{NEF/H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ can lead to the crystallization of CPF-1. CPF-1 consists of $4_{1}$ -helical rod SBUs linked by benzene units of the bptc to produce 1D cylindrical nanotubular channels with the diameter around $1.0\ \mathrm{nm}$ . The desolvated CPF-1 exhibits a moderate BET surface area of $853~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ and $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ uptake of $1.29\mathrm{wt\%}$ at $77\mathrm{K}$ and 1 atm, and selectively adsorbs $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ over ${\bf N}_{2}$ .
Lah and Zou et al.111 used bis(3,5-dicarboxyphenyl) terephthalamide $\left(\mathrm{H}_{4}\mathrm{A}23\right)$ to construct a 3D porous Co-based MOF $\left[\mathrm{Co}_{2}\mathrm{A}23(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{3}\right]$ . The framework is composed of the dinuclear $\mathrm{Co}_{2}(\mathrm{COO})_{4}$ SBUs linked by the organic ligands, forming a 3D network with a lon-derived topology where the SBU and the ligand serve as 4-connected tetrahedral nodes. It is well known that for regular tetrahedral nodes, there are two possible types of topologies, namely, diamond (dia) and lonsdaleite (lon) nets. Although the two structures are composed of fused 6-membered rings, all such rings in diamond have the chair conformation while those in the lonsdaleite assume both chair and boat conformations. In the case above, the preferential formation of both chair and boat conformations in fused 6-membered rings might benefit from the amide groups increasing the flexibility of the ligand. The desolvated sample exhibited a BET surface area of $106.6\ \mathrm{m}^{2}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , which is significantly lower than the one calculated from the single-crystal structure, indicating that the framework underwent partial collapse or severe distortion during the activation. Moreover, the desolvated solid showed the hysteretic $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ sorption behavior, which is ascribed to strong interactions of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ molecules with the host solid or the apparent structural changes upon uptake of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ .
Compared with the widely investigated homometallic microporous MOFs, the heterometallic microporous MOFs were relatively less reported. An unusual heterometallic microporous MOF $\mathbf{Cu}_{3}\mathbf{Cr}(\mathbf{sbtc})_{2}$ was synthesized by a solvothermal reaction of $\mathrm{CrCl}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}3\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and trans-stilbene- $^{3,3^{\prime},5,5^{\prime}}$ -tetracarboxylic acid ligand $\left(\mathrm{H}_{4}\mathrm{sbtc},\mathrm{H}_{4}\mathrm{A}2\right)$ .112 The structure of the compound is similar to that of PCN-11 except that there exist two kinds of paddlewheel SBUs, $\mathrm{{Cu}_{2}(\mathrm{{COO})_{4}}}$ and $\mathrm{{CuCr}(C O O)_{4}}$ , in the framework. After desolvation, the compound exhibits a high $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ adsorption heat of $7.0{-}8.4\mathrm{kJ}\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ , leading to a significant $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ uptake of $2.7\mathrm{wt\%}$ at $77\mathrm{K}$ and 1 bar, which reaches a saturation of $5.0\mathrm{wt\%}$ at 28 bar. The total uptake is $6.0~\mathrm{wt\%}$ at $77~\mathrm{K}$ and 60 bar.
## 2.2 Heterogenetic catalysis
A 3D porous $\mathrm{In\big(m\big)}$ -based anionic MOF was synthesized by a solvothermal reaction of the semirigid ligand, $^{5,5^{\prime}}$ -methylenediisophthalic acid ( $\mathrm{\tilde{{H}}}_{4}$ mdip, $\mathrm{H}_{4}\mathrm{A}32\$ ), with $\mathrm{In}\big(\mathrm{NO}_{3}\big)_{3}{\cdot}4.5\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}.$ .113 The compound consists of the mononuclear $[\mathrm{In}(\mathrm{COO})_{4}]$ nodes bridged by the tetracarboxylates to form a 3D 4-connected uninodal network where both the $\mathrm{In}^{3+}$ ion and the mdip ligand serve as 4-connected nodes. There exist two types of rhombic channels along the $b$ axis where the disordered counterions $\mathrm{{Me}}_{2}{\mathrm{NH}}_{2}^{+}$ ions reside. Because of the Lewis acidity of the $\mathrm{In}^{3+}$ ions, the catalytic activities were examined in the Lewis acidpromoted Friedel–Crafts alkylation of pyrrole with nitrostyrenes. Compared to the homogeneous catalyst $\mathrm{In}{\left(\mathrm{NO}_{3}\right)}_{3}{\cdot}21/2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ , the reaction in water gave comparable conversion and higher yield, and the substrate scope is very broad in terms of nitrostyrenes. Furthermore, the compound can be easily recycled by simple filtration and reused three times without obvious loss of the catalytic activity.
Aromatic azoliums are precursors to NHC (N-heterocyclic carbine), which are excellent ligands for transition metals and unique Lewis base catalysts. Thus, Hupp et al.114 incorporated an imidazolium tetracarboxylate ( $\mathrm{\ddot{H}}_{4}\mathrm{\dot{l}m t a}$ , $\mathrm{{H_{4}A42}}$ ) into a microporous MOF material via a solvothermal reaction of $\mathrm{H}_{4}\mathrm{imta}$ and $\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}2.5\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}.$ In the structure, each imta ligand is linked to four other imta ligands via the dicopper paddlewheel to form hexagonal channels into which the $\mathbf{C}_{2}$ carbon of the imidazolium is projected. The gas sorption properties are highly dependent on the activation temperature and time. Heating under vacuum at $473\mathrm{~K~}$ for $^{4\mathrm{h}}$ produces the optimally activated sample, exhibiting a good $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ uptake capacity of $1.8\mathrm{mmol}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ and high IAST $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ adsorption selectivity of 20 at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ and 1 bar. The availability of OMSs and the charged azolium struts and counterions might be the main contributing factors for such high $\mathrm{CO_{2}/C H_{4}}$ selectivity because these two elements create polar cavities that preferentially adsorb $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ because of its quadrupole moment. However, the catalytic properties concerning NHC were not explored.
It is well known that metalloporphyrins exhibit unique catalytic, electronic and optical properties. Thus, five porphyrinbased MOFs $[\mathrm{Cu}_{2}(\mathrm{Mddcpp})$ , $\mathbf{M}\ =\ \mathrm{Zn}^{2+}$ , $\mathrm{\bfNi}^{2+}$ , $\mathrm{Pd}^{2+}$ , ${\bf M n}^{3+}({\bf N O}_{3});$ $\mathrm{Ru}^{2+}(\mathrm{CO})]$ were synthesized by the reaction of $\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}3\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ with the corresponding tetracarboxylic acid building block Mddcpp (M-A27). These porous complexes are isostructural, regardless of the identity of the central metal of the ddcpp moiety, adopting a structure similar to that of NOTT-109 and MMPF-1 due to steric congestion at the Mddcpp moiety.67,87 Their permanent porosities have been established using gas sorption isotherms. The use of THF as the activation solvent led to the optimal result among typical activation solvents. The surface areas were smaller than that of NOTT-109 because the guest DMF molecules in the cage could not be completely removed due to the smaller cage aperture compared to that of NOTT-109. In fact, weight loss corresponding to the loss of DMF molecules was still observed for a sample even after Soxhlet extraction with THF. Of the five complexes, $[\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\{\mathrm{Ru(CO)ddcpp}\}]$ has the largest BET surface area and $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ uptake despite the axially coordinated groups. Also, their catalytic performance was evaluated in cyclopropanation of alkenes. All five MOFs showed catalytic activity, but partial collapse of the frameworks occurred during the reaction. Nevertheless, this work highlights the fact that using metalloporphyrins as MOF building blocks is an effective method for incorporating accessible metal sites (AMSs) into MOFs. To expand the pore window and interior space, the same group also synthesized an elongated but geometrically equivalent ddcpp analogue, 5,15-bis(dicarboxybiphenyl)porphyrin (bdcbpp, A31), and used it to construct a new porphyrin-based MOF $[\mathrm{Cu}_{2}(\mathrm{Znbdcbpp})]$ .115 The 3D structure of $[\mathrm{Cu}_{2}(\mathrm{Znbdcbpp})]$ is very similar to that of the above ddcppbased MOF $[\mathrm{Cu}_{2}(\mathrm{Znddcpp})]$ . Compared with $[\mathrm{Cu}_{2}(\mathrm{Znddcpp})],$ $[\mathbf{Cu}_{2}(\mathbf{Znbdcbpp})]$ contains larger pore windows along the $a$ and $b$ axes due to the incorporation of the elongated organic linker and their different orientations. Although the microporous framework structure was unstable with respect to the removal of the guest molecules in the cavity, these larger pore windows might facilitate solution based heterogeneous catalysis.
A metal metalloporphyrin polyhedral cage based MOF, MMPF-3, was prepared solvothermally from a custom-designed porphyrin ligand, 5,15-bi(3,5-dicarboxyphenyl)-10,20-bis(2,6- dibromophenyl)porphyrin (dcdbp, A28) and $\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}$ .116 MMPF-3 is based on molecular building blocks (MBBs) $\mathrm{{Co}_{2}(\upmu_{2}\mathrm{{-}H_{2}O)(\upmu_{2}O)_{4}\mathrm{{-}}}}$ $(\mathrm{COO})_{4}$ that afford the cubohemioctahedral SBBs serving as 12-connected nodes in the resulting fcu topology network. There exist three types of polyhedral cages: a cubohemioctahedron, a truncated tetrahedron and a truncated octahedron (Fig. 6a–c). The permanent porosity was confirmed by $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption at $273\mathrm{~K~}$ with a surface area of about $750\ \mathrm{m}^{2}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ . Given the high density of cobalt metal centers $\left(1.23\mathrm{\nm}^{-3}\right.$ , its catalytic performance was evaluated in epoxidation of transstilbene using tert-butyl hydroperoxide (TBHP) as the oxidant in acetonitrile at $333\mathrm{~K~}$ . Control experiments show that in terms of both selectivity and overall conversion, MMPF-3 is more efficient than the homogeneous cobalt(II) metallated dcdbp and other cobalt based MOFs examined such as fcu-MOF-1,117 MMPF- $2^{118}$ and $\mathbf{PPF-1CO}^{119}$ (Fig. 6d), which is attributed to the higher density of active cobalt centers and/or cooperative interactions between the active centers in MMPF-3. In addition, MMPF-3 can be reused for eight cycles without significant drop in its catalytic activity.
Proline and its derivatives are well known asymmetric organocatalysts. Using $\mathrm{L^{-}}$ and D-pyrrolidine-2-yl-imidazoles as chiral adducts, Duan et al.120 synthesized two enantiomorphs Ce-MDIP1 and Ce-MDIP2 having AMSs pointing towards 1D chiral channels. Solvothermal reaction of methylenediisophthalic acid $\mathrm{\tilde{{H}}}_{4}$ mdip, $\mathbf{H_{4}A}32$ ) and $\mathrm{Ce}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{3}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ in the presence of L-N-tert-butoxylcarbonyl-2-imidazole-1-pyrrolidine (L-BCIP) in water gave the compound Ce-MDIP1. In the structure, the mdip ligands link the cerium ions through the carboxyl groups to form a non-interpenetrating 3D network containing 1D irregular chiral channels with a $10.5\ \times\ 6.0\ \mathring{\mathbf{A}}^{2}$ cross-section along the $a$ axis. The cerium ions have removable coordinated water molecules which are well positioned within the channels, and therefore have the potential to function as a Lewis acid catalyst. The asymmetric catalytic activities were assessed in cyanosilylation of carbonyl compounds. Ce-MDIP1 exhibited a high conversion and excellent enantioselectivity, and could be easily recovered by simple filtration and reused at least three times with a slight decrease in the reactivity and enantioselectivity.
A homochiral porous MOF decorated with chiral dihydroxyl recognition sites was constructed from an enantiopure ligand, $^{3,3^{\prime}}$ -di-tert-butyl- $^{5,5^{\prime}}$ -di(3,5-dicarboxyphenyl)- $^{6,6^{\prime}}$ -dimethyl- $\cdot1.1^{\prime}.$ - biphenyl- $^{\cdot2,2^{\prime}}$ -diol $\mathbf{(H_{4}A43)}$ , and $\mathrm{Dy}^{3+}/\mathrm{Na}^{+}$ ions.121 Heating a mixture of (S)- $\mathbf{\cdotH_{4}A4}3$ , $\mathrm{DyCl}_{3}{\cdot}6\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ and NaCl in DMF, HOAc and water at $373\mathrm{~K~}$ afforded single crystals of $\mathrm{\small{[DyNaL(H_{2}O)_{4}]{\cdot6}H_{2}O}}$ [(S)-1]. In the structure, the adjacent Dy and Na atoms are connected by carboxylate groups to generate a left-handed helical chain with a helical pitch of $14.1273\mathring\mathrm{A}$ running along the $c$ axis. Four adjacent helices are bridged by biphenyl backbones of the ligands to form a nanotube with an open size of $\sim1.81\ \times$ $1.81\ \mathrm{nm}^{2}$ . The two hydroxyl groups of the ligand are partly shielded from the open channels by the biphenyl rings, but are still accessible to guest molecules. Due to the presence of large chiral pores and available OH groups, the enantioselective separation capabilities were evaluated. (S)-1 exhibited remarkable selective inclusion of the L enantiomers over the D enantiomers for racemic mandelate derivatives (Table 1). The adsorbent can be recovered and reused without the deterioration of enantioselectivity for at least three cycles. Also, the photoelectrocyclization of tropolone ethers within (S)-1 was examined. After irradiation of the inclusion adducts at $365~\mathrm{{nm}}$ for $10\ \mathrm{min}$ , the products were obtained in $>90\%$ yield with ee of $98.5\%$ for tropolone methyl phenyl ether and $83.3\%$ for tropolone ethyl phenyl ether, respectively. The high selectivity may originate from the porous structure coupled with an amphiphilic channel interior lined with chiral OH groups.
The enantioselective ring-opening of meso-epoxides by aromatic amines was achieved using a homochiral MOF UTSA-32,122 which was obtained via a solvothermal reaction of (S)- $^{-6,6^{\prime}}$ -dichloro- $^{2,2^{\prime}\mathrm{.}}$ diethoxyl- $^{1,1^{\prime}}$ -binaphthyl- $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -bis(5-isophthalic acid) $\left((S)–\mathrm{H}_{4}\mathrm{A}22\right)$ , and $\mathrm{Zn}(\mathbf{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}.$ The dinuclear $\mathrm{Zn_{2}(C O O)_{4}}$ clusters are bridged by the organic linkers to form a 3D framework in which there exist 1D irregular pores about $3.2\times10.0\mathring{\mathbf{A}}^{2}$ along the $a$ axis. The corresponding $\alpha$ -hydroxyamines were obtained in good to excellent yields with mediocre to excellent ee values. The reactivity and enantioselectivity originate from the Lewis acidity of open metal nodes formed after activation, which are immobilized in the homochiral framework.
It is well known that the homogeneous hydrogen-bonddonating (HBD) catalyst is prone to undergo self-quenching, thus decreasing the catalyst solubility and reactivity. If the HBD catalyst is incorporated into the porous MOFs, such issues might be addressed. Roberts et al. reported the first such example of a MOF-based HBD catalyst,123 which is highly active for Friedel–Crafts reactions between pyrroles and nitroalkenes. Combining 1,3-di(3,5-dicarboxyphenyl)urea $\mathrm{(H}_{4}\mathrm{A}36\mathrm{)}$ , $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -bipyridine, and $\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ under solvothermal conditions yielded a new microporous MOF named as NU-601 with non-aggregative $\mathbf{N}{\mathrm{-}}\mathbf{H}$ urea moieties projecting into the pores (Fig. 7a). However, the urea moieties are occupied by DMF solvent molecules via hydrogen bonding. To open the urea sites, it is necessary to remove the hydrogen bonded DMF, which can be easily achieved by solvent exchange with $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NO}_{2}$ . No conversion was observed in the Friedel–Crafts reaction between $N_{\mathbf{\lambda}}$ -methylpyrrole and $(E)$ -1-nitroprop-1-ene with $10~\mathrm{\mol\%}$ of NU-601 in toluene; however, NU-601 shows high reaction activity in a mixed solvent of THF and $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NO}_{2}$ . The results might be ascribed to the product inhibition in the non-polar solvent. More importantly, NU-601 is a more active catalyst for Friedel–Crafts reactions compared to the homogeneous catalyst 1,3-diphenylurea, and exhibits the reagent size selectivity (Fig. 7b). The catalyst can be reused five times with only a slight degradation of reactivity.
## 2.3 Luminescent sensing
Liang et al. synthesized a family of lanthanide MOFs with rutile topology using the ligand, $^{5,5^{\prime}}$ -(1H-1,2,3-triazole-1,4-diyl)-diisophthalic acid $\mathbf{(H_{4}A41)}$ .124 In these compounds, the dinuclear building blocks were linked by the ligand to form a 3D network where the triazole is uncoordinated. The Tb-based compound exhibits good selective luminescent sensing behavior to $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ ions in aqueous solution. When the Tb-based compound was immersed in an aqueous solution of different metal salts, no significant change in luminescence intensity was observed for $\mathbf{K}^{+}$ , while slight luminescence quenching was found for ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ , $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ , $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Zn}}^{2+}$ , $\mathrm{In}^{3+}$ and $\operatorname{Cd}^{2+}$ ions. Most notably, the luminescent intensity was selectively quenched by ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ ions. The selectivity and sensitivity of luminescence quenching are probably induced by the highly selective coordination ability of the uncoordinated carboxylic acids and the triazole groups in the compound.
A novel 3D calcium based MOF based on a naphthalene diimide chromophore has been synthesized via a solvothermal reaction of $^{5,5^{\prime}}$ -(1,3,6,8-tetraoxobenzo[lmn][3,8] phenanthroline2,7-diyl)bis-1,3-benzenedicarboxylate $\mathbf{\left(H_{4}A25\right)}$ and $\mathrm{Ca}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}4\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ .125 In the structure, the ${\mathrm{Ca}}^{2+}$ ions are bridged by carboxylate groups to form a 1D $\mathbf{4_{1}}$ helical chain with a helical pitch of $10.437\mathring{\textbf{A}}$ , which is further cross-linked to four adjacent chains through organic naphthalenediimide (NDI) moieties, leading to the formation of a 3D network. Due to the large voids of the framework, two such networks interpenetrate each other by strong intermolecular $\pi{-}\pi$ stacking interaction between the $\pi$ -deficient NDI rings. Interestingly, the compound undergoes a photochromic transformation from yellowish to dark green upon irradiation by sunlight, and can return to the original color in the dark. The photoinduced radical generation together with $\pi{-}\pi$ electron transfer might be responsible for such photo-responsive behaviors.
## 3. Multifunctional MOFs based on tri-isophthalates
Scheme 3 summarizes a variety of $C_{3}$ -symmetrical hexacarboxylate linkers containing three isophthalate moieties at the end, which have been incorporated into porous MOFs.
## 3.1 Copper tri-isophthalate frameworks
Lah et al. synthesized a $C_{3}$ -symmetric hexacarboxylic acid containing three isophthalate units, 1,3,5-tris(3,5-dicarboxyphenylethynyl)benzene $\mathbf{(H_{6}B3)}$ , and used it to construct two isostructural MOFs (PMOF-2(Zn) and PMOF- $2(\mathbf{Cu}))$ .126 Briefly, PMOF- $2(\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{n})$ was synthesized by a solvothermal reaction of $\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ with the ligand in DMF, while the copper analogue, PMOF- $2(\mathbf{Cu})$ was obtained by a solvothermal reaction of $\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}3\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and the ligand in the presence of a small amount of HCl. In the structures, the isophthalate units of the ligand are linked with in situ formed paddlewheel SBUs to form the cuboctahedral SBBs, which are covalently linked through the 5-positions of the isophthalate moieties by the 1,3,5-benzenetriyl-tris(ethynyl) part of the ligand to give rise to a (3,24)-coordinated rhtbased net, where the ligand and cuboctahedron are taken as 3- and 24-connected nodes, respectively (we see below that a more logical, and informative description of this topology is as a (3,4)-coordinated trinodal net ntt). Alternatively, the framework can be viewed as the face-centered cubic (fcc) packing of cuboctahedra in 3D space, which leads to two additional different kinds of polyhedral cages: one a truncated tetrahedron and the other a truncated octahedron. As shown in Fig. 8, the cuboctahedron (cub- $\vec{\mathbf{\nabla}}O_{\mathbf{h}_{.}}\mathbf{\dot{\sigma}}$ ) was composed of 24 isophthalates units from B3 ligands and 12 dicopper paddlewheel units. The truncated tetrahedral cage $\left(\mathrm{{T}}{-}T_{\mathrm{{d}}}\right)$ was formed by 4 B3 linkers and 12 dicopper paddlewheel units. The truncated octahedron $\left(\mathrm{T}{\cdot}O_{\mathrm{h}}\right)$ was composed of 8 B3 ligands and 24 dicopper paddlewheel units. One truncated tetrahedron is connected to 4 cuboctahedra, and one truncated octahedron is connected to 6 cuboctahedra, by sharing the triangular and square windows of a cuboctahedron, respectively, leading to a highly porous framework. Although these two MOFs are isostructural, their stabilities and sorption behaviors are distinctly different. When the samples were activated, PMOF-2(Cu) remained intact while PMOF- $2(\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{n})$ was not stable, losing its crystallinity. The activated PMOF-2(Cu) exhibits a BET surface area of $3700\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , significantly higher than that of the activated PMOF- $2(\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{n})(72\mathbf{m}^{2}\mathbf{g}^{-1})$ . Also, the PMOF$2({\bf C u})$ shows high $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ adsorption of $2.29\ \mathrm{wt\%}$ at $77~\mathrm{K}$ and 1 atm, reaching the maximum value of $5.0~\mathrm{wt\%}$ at around 30 bar. The total $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ uptake is $7.0~\mathrm{wt\%}$ at $77\mathrm{~K~}$ and 50 bar. Interestingly, the zinc ions in PMOF- $2(\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{n})$ can be entirely replaced by $\operatorname{Cu}(\operatorname{II})$ ions via postsynthetic transmetalation. The complete transmetalation led to full recovery of the sorption capacities.127 Clearly, this study indicates that construction of metal–organic polyhedron based MOFs is a very effective route to obtain the MOFs with high surface area and large $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ storage capacities.
With this strategy, solvothermal treatment of an elongated hexacarboxylate ligand, 1,3,5-tris( $_{3^{\prime},5^{\prime}}$ -dicarboxy[1, $1^{\prime}$ -biphenyl]- 4-yl)benzene $\left(\mathrm{H}_{6}\mathrm{B}12\right)$ , with $\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}3\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ yielded a rht-based MOF NOTT-112.128 Similar to PMOF-2, the overall structure of NOTT-112 can be viewed as the packing of three types of polyhedral cages, namely, cub- ${\cdot}O_{\mathrm{h}}$ , $\mathrm{T}{\cdot}T_{\mathrm{d}}$ and $\mathrm{T}{\cdot}O_{\mathrm{h}}$ , in the ratio of $1:2:1$ in 3D space (Fig. 9a). The hierarchical porous structural feature is also reflected in the ${\bf N}_{2}$ adsorption isotherm collected at $77~\mathrm{K}$ showing small changes of slope in the relatively low pressure region, indicating that the different sized cavities are filled in sequence as the pressure increases. The fully evacuated framework NOTT-112a exhibits a high BET surface area of $3800~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ as well as excellent low-pressure and high-pressure $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ adsorption capacities (Fig. 9b): total $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ uptake of $2.3~\mathrm{wt\%}$ under 1 bar, and $10.0\mathrm{wt\%}$ under 77 bar at $77\mathrm{K}.$ . This efficiency in adsorbing $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ at low pressures can be attributed to the presence of exposed copper sites within the relatively small cuboctahedral cage, which has been experimentally confirmed by neutron powder diffraction (NPD) studies of ${\bf D}_{2}$ adsorption in desolvated NOTT-112a revealing that the exposed copper sites within the smallest cuboctahedral cages are the primary binding sites for ${\bf D}_{2}$ in this material.129 The ${\bf D}_{2}\mathrm{-}{\bf C}{\bf u}$ distance of $2.23\mathring{\mathrm{~A~}}$ is shorter than the ${\bf D}_{2}\mathrm{-}{\bf C u}$ distance of $2.39\mathring{\mathrm{~A~}}$ observed in HKUST-1,130 clearly indicating that a specific geometrical arrangement of exposed $\mathrm{{Cu}(\mathrm{{II})}}$ sites can strengthen the interactions between ${\bf D}_{2}$ molecules and OMSs. In fact, a similar cage has also been found in PCN-12,88 which exhibits an exceptionally high $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ uptake of $3.05\ \mathrm{wt\%}$ at $77\mathrm{~K~}$ and 1 bar. The efficient high-pressure performance was attributed to its high surface area and pore volume. Thus, this study suggests that mixing of small and large polyhedral cages with open copper sites represents a powerful strategy to optimize $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ uptake over extended pressure ranges.
Later, the same group synthesized two expanded MOFs NOTT-116 and NOTT-119,129,131 which are isoreticular with the above-mentioned NOTT-112. As expected, increasing the size of the central part of the ligand enlarges the sizes of the $\mathrm{{T}}{\cdot}O_{\mathrm{h}}$ and $\mathrm{T}{\cdot}T_{\mathrm{d}},$ but does not change the size of the cub- $O_{\mathrm{h}}$ . NOTT-116 incorporating an elongated nanosized ligand $\mathrm{H}_{6}\mathrm{B}6$ within the framework shows a higher BET surface area of $4664\mathrm{~m}^{2}\mathrm{~g}^{-1}$ relative to NOTT-112, and a total $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ adsorption capacity of $9.2~\mathrm{wt\%}$ at $77\mathrm{~K~}$ and 50 bar.129 NOTT-119 was based on a more extended nanosized hexacarboxylate linker $\mathrm{H}_{6}\mathrm{B}14$ .131 Intriguingly, the ${\bf N}_{2}$ isotherm for the activated NOTT-119a shows typical Type-IV behavior with a slight adsorption–desorption hysteresis, which might be due to the unique hierarchical polyhedral structure. The flexible nature of the framework is another possible explanation proposed by Zhou et al.132 Compared to NOTT-116a, NOTT-119a has a higher pore volume but lower surface area $(4118\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ . NOTT-119a shows a high total $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ uptake of $9.2\mathrm{wt\%}$ at $77\mathrm{K}$ and 60 bar, and total $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ uptake of $165\mathrm{cm}^{3}\left(\mathrm{STP}\right)\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ and 80 bar. From the $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ adsorption data summarized in Table 2, it can be seen that when the pore volume increases from NOTT-112a to NOTT-119a, the maximum excess adsorption a $P_{\mathrm{max}}$ indicates the pressure where the maximum excess $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ adsorption at 77 K was reached. b $C_{\mathrm{sat}}$ corresponds to the saturated excess $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ adsorption capacity at $77~\mathrm{K}$ .
pressure first decreases and then increases, while the saturated excess $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ adsorption capacity decreases, indicating that simply increasing the available pore volume cannot secure an increase in gas uptake, and therefore there exists an optimum pore size and geometry for $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ storage. When the size of the linker is further extended beyond the one in NOTT-119, the network is no longer stable against the conventional thermal activation. For instance, the isostructural rht-based MOF NU-100,133 also known as PCN-610,134 which incorporates a slightly longer ligand $\mathrm{(H_{6}B8)}$ than that in NOTT-119, undergoes complete loss of porosity upon thermal desolvation under vacuum. However, this mesoporous network retains crystallinity on activation using the mild supercritical $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ drying (SCD) method. The resulting NU-100 exhibits an ultrahigh surface area of $6143\mathrm{~m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ and the excellent gas sorption capacities for $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ and $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ . The maximum excess $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ adsorption capacity is $9.95~\mathrm{wt\%}$ at $77~\mathrm{K}$ and 56 bar, which is the highest one reported thus far.32 The total $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ storage capacity for NU-100 at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ and 40 bar is $52.61\mathrm{mmol}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , one of the largest values yet reported for MOF materials.
To investigate the effects of pore surface decoration on gas adsorption, the same group synthesized a series of isoreticular frameworks based on the (3,24)-coordinated rht net: NOTT-113, NOTT-114 and NOTT-115, which are constructed from dicopper paddlewheel SBUs and the hexacarboxylate linkers $\mathbf{H}_{6}\mathbf{B}4$ , $\mathbf{H}_{6}\mathbf{B}5$ and $\mathbf{H}_{6}\mathbf{B}13$ , respectively.135 Similar to NOTT-112, the exposed copper centers inside the cuboctahedral cages significantly contribute to their high $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ adsorption capacities at $77~\mathrm{K}$ and 1 bar (2.39, 2.28 and $2.42~\mathrm{wt\%}$ for NOTT-113, NOTT-114 and NOTT-115, respectively). Of three MOFs studied, NOTT-115 exhibits the highest $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ uptakes at $77~\mathrm{K}$ and 60 bar $(6.7~\mathrm{wt\%})$ for NOTT-113, $6.8~\mathrm{wt\%}$ for NOTT-114 and $7.5~\mathrm{wt\%}$ for NOTT115), because NOTT-115 has the largest pore volume. However, NOTT-113 and NOTT-114 exhibit very similar $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ storage capacities, despite NOTT-114 having both a higher surface area and a larger pore volume than NOTT-113. This indicates that the amine-substituted central core leads to less favorable interaction with $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ than the other ligand systems studied. Comparison of the heat of $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ adsorption in the three MOFs $(5.9\mathrm{~kJ}\mathrm{\mol}^{-1}$ for NOTT-113, $5.3\mathrm{\kJ\mol^{-1}}$ for NOTT-114 and $5.8~\mathrm{kJ~mol}^{-1}$ for NOTT-115 at zero coverage) also reveals that amine functionalization of the conjugated ligand core in NOTT114 does reduce the interaction energy between $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ and the framework, while functionalization of the central core of the hexagonal face with more aromatic rings increases $\mathbf{H}_{2}.$ –framework interactions.
Independently, Zhou’s group134,136 also constructed an isoreticular series of rht-based MOFs PCN-61, PCN-66, PCN-68, which are made from the dicopper paddlewheel SBUs and hexatopic hexacarboxylic acids $\mathbf{H}_{6}\mathbf{B}3$ , $\mathbf{H}_{6}\mathbf{B}7$ , and $\mathrm{H}_{6}\mathrm{B}6$ , respectively. Virtually, PCN-61 and PCN-68 are the same as PMOF$2({\bf C u})$ and NOTT-116, respectively. Generally, higher surface areas of isoreticular MOFs can be obtained by ligand extension but they tend to collapse upon activation; however, high BET surface areas were observed for PCN-61, PCN-66, and PCN-68, which the authors think is due to the fact that the formed cuboctahedra stabilized the frameworks by incorporating mesocavities with the microwindows. Of the three frameworks, PCN-61 has the smallest pore size and therefore exhibits the highest heat of $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ adsorption and the highest $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ adsorption capacity of $2.25~\mathrm{wt\%}$ at $77\mathrm{~K~}$ and 1 bar, which is in good agreement with that of PMOF-2(Cu). PCN-68 has the highest surface area and thus the highest total $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ uptake capacity of $13\ \mathrm{wt\%}$ at $77\mathrm{~K~}$ and 100 bar. The total $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ storage capacities at $298\mathrm{K}$ and 65 bar are 219, 187, and $187\mathrm{cm}^{3}\left(\mathrm{STP}\right)\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ for PCN61, PCN-66 and PCN-68, respectively. The total $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption capacities at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ and 35 bar are 25.8, 28.9 and $34.2\mathrm{mmol}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ for PCN-61, PCN-66 and PCN-68, respectively. Later on, the same group also synthesized one more (3,24)-connected rht-based mesoporous MOF PCN-69,132 also known as NOTT-119,131 via a solvothermal reaction of $\mathrm{H}_{6}\mathrm{B}14$ and $\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}3\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ in DMF. The ${\bf N}_{2}$ sorption isotherm exhibits a pseudo type-I isotherm with a noticeable hysteresis, characteristic of a mesoporous MOF. Compared with PCN-68, PCN-69 has a lower BET surface area because of more pores distributing in the mesoporous range, despite a similar amount of adsorbed ${\bf N}_{2}$ . Surprisingly, PCN-69 has a high heat of $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ adsorption $\left(8.14\mathrm{kJ}\mathrm{mol}^{-1}\right.$ at low coverage), presumably because the bent ligand creates pockets and concaves with improved binding affinity toward $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ . However, the high pressure $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ uptake capacity in PCN-69 is not as good as PCN-68, indicating that a larger portion of mesoporosity is not good for high-pressure $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ storage. Interestingly, the gate-opening sorption behavior was observed, indicative of its framework flexibility. Such a framework flexibility originates from the pressure-responsive curvature change of the ligand adopted. A high gate opening pressure was observed in PCN-69, revealing that a higher energy is needed to trigger the curvature change, consistent with the origin of the framework flexibility.
Solvothermal reaction of $\mathrm{H}_{6}\mathrm{B}10$ and $\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}2.5\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ in DMF/EtOH/HCl at $353~\mathrm{~K~}$ gave a rht-based MOF NU-111 (Fig. 10a).137,138 The ${\bf N}_{2}$ sorption isotherm reveals that NU-111 has a BET surface area of $5000\ \mathrm{m}^{2}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , which is higher than that of PCN-69/NOTT-119, although the linker adopted in the latter is longer. This suggests that replacing the phenyl spacers of the organic linker with triple bond spacers can effectively boost the molecule accessible gravimetric surface areas of MOF. At 77 K and 110 bar, the total $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ uptake of NU-111 reaches up to $13.5~\mathrm{wt\%}$ (Fig. 10b). Also, NU-111 exhibits a significant $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption of 38 mmol ${\mathrm{g}}^{-1}$ at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ and 30 bar. The total $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ uptake at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ and 65 bar by NU-111 reaches $0.36\mathrm{~g~g~}^{-1}$ corresponding to a volumetric uptake of $205~\mathrm{cm}^{3}$ (STP) $\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ . Both values are about $75\%$ of the DOE’s new target for $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ storage, indicating NU-111 is unique in its equally good volumetric and gravimetric uptakes. The $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ storage working capacity, defined as the difference in uptakes between the pressures of 65 bar and 5 bar, is $\mathrm{177~cm^{3}}$ (STP) $\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ . Along this line, the same group synthesized two more new MOFs NU-109 and NU-110 using the organic linkers $\mathbf{H}_{6}\mathbf{B}11$ and $\mathbf{H}_{6}\mathbf{B}9$ , respectively, which display the highest experimental BET surface area of any porous materials reported to date (7010 and ${7140~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}}$ for NU-109 and NU-110, respectively).139
To elucidate the effect of the amide groups upon $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption, Zheng et al.140 constructed a rht-based MOF Cu-TPBTM based on dicopper paddlewheel SBUs and a hexacarboxylate ligand containing acylamide groups, $^{5,5^{\prime},5^{\prime\prime}}$ -[1,3,5- benzenetriyltris(carbonylimino)]tris-1,3-benzene dicarboxylic acid $\mathbf{\left(H_{6}T P B T M\right.}$ , $\mathbf{H}_{6}\mathbf{B}18$ , Fig. 11a). The excess $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ uptake reaches $330~\mathrm{cm}^{3}$ (STP) $\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ and 20 bar, approaching the performance of MIL-101 (MIL represents ‘‘Materials of Institut Lavoisier’’).141 Most remarkably, this MOF exhibits a stronger binding affinity for $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ as well as a better $\mathbf{CO}_{2}/\mathbf{N}_{2}$ selectivity over the total pressure range measured (0–20 bar) than does the isostructural analogue PCN-61 (Fig. 11b). Note that this MOF and PCN-61 possess similar pore size, surface area, and the density of open copper sites, and the only difference between this MOF and PCN-61 is the substitution of the $\mathbf{C}{\equiv}\mathbf{C}$ moiety with an amide moiety. Thus, the better performance of this MOF in $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ capture relative to PCN-61 is believed to be due to the large dipole–quadrupole interactions between the amide groups in this MOF and $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ and/or $\mathbf{NH\cdot\cdot\cdotO}\mathrm{=c}{\mathrm{=}}\mathbf{O}$ hydrogen bonds. To further demonstrate the advantages of amide groups for $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ capture, the same group synthesized two expanded isoreticular rht-based MOFs $\left(\left[\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{B}19)\right]\right)$ and $\left[\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathbf{B}20)\right])$ built from nanosized hexacarboxylate linkers $\mathbf{H}_{6}\mathbf{B}19$ and $\mathbf{H}_{6}\mathbf{B}20$ .142 PXRD and gas sorption studies revealed that both materials are flexible, different from the parent MOF Cu-TPBTM. Despite the fact that the surface of the MOF $\left[\mathrm{Cu}_{3}\big(\mathrm{B}20\big)\right]$ is decorated with nitrogen-containing triazine rings, it has almost the same gas sorption behavior as $\begin{array}{r}{[\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{B}19)].}\end{array}$ . At $273\mathrm{~K~}$ and 20 bar, the MOF $\left[\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{B}19)\right]$ takes up an exceptionally high excess $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ uptake of $157~\mathrm{wt\%}$ but very limited amounts of $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ $(13~\mathrm{wt\%})$ and ${\bf N}_{2}$ $(11~\mathrm{wt\%})$ . The Henry’s law selectivities of $\mathrm{CO_{2}/C H_{4}}$ and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ for $\left[\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{B}19)\right]$ reach up to 8.6 and 34.3, much higher than the corresponding values of MOF-177 (4.4 and 17.5) and most other MOF materials, indicating that the introduction of amide groups into MOFs did enhance the adsorption selectivity. The results were further supported by Grand Canonical Monte Carlo (GCMC) simulations and the first principle calculations. GCMC simulations reveal that $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ molecules in $\left[\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{L}19)\right]$ are mainly embedded in the relatively small cuboctahedral cage, and $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ molecules prefer to locate at both the open copper sites and CONH groups within the framework. Moreover, the firstprinciple calculation indicates that the $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ binding energy of the carbonyl sites is very comparable with that of open copper sites, while the $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ affinity of the amide sites is significantly weaker than that of the carbonyl sites. So the studies highlight that immobilization of polar amide functionalities into frameworks might be an effective approach to enhance $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ capture capacity and selectivity.
In order to introduce nitrogen-rich units into highly porous MOFs for enhancing the gas uptake capacity, Li et al.143 constructed an rht-based MOF Cu-TDPAT built on a hexacarboxylate ligand with an imino triazine backbone, 2,4,6-tris(3,5-dicarboxylphenylamino)-1,3,5-triazine ( $\mathrm{\tilde{H}_{6}T D P A T}$ , $\mathbf{H}_{6}\mathbf{B}21\dot{}$ ) (Fig. 12a). Cu-TDPAT is the smallest member among (3,24)-connected rht-based MOFs reported to date, featuring a high density of both OMSs $\left(1.76\mathrm{nm}^{-3}\right).$ and Lewis basic sites (LBSs, $3.52\mathrm{nm}^{-3}$ . Remarkably, such a dual functionalization of the framework, namely, concurrent incorporation of high density of OMSs and LBSs, results in its high $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ uptake capacity and strong adsorption enthalpy for $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ , as well as high adsorption selectivity of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ over ${\bf N}_{2}$ at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ (Fig. 12b). At $298\ \mathrm{K},$ , the $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ uptake amount is 132 and $31.3~\mathrm{cm}^{3}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at 1.0 bar and 0.1 bar, respectively, substantially higher than all previously reported rhtbased structures. A very high adsorption enthalpy of $42.2\mathrm{kJ}\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ at zero loading is clearly indicative of strong adsorbate–adsorbent interactions. The IAST $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ separation selectivity as high as 79 was achieved for a $10:90$ $\mathbf{CO}_{2}–\mathbf{N}_{2}$ gas mixture at a total pressure of 1 atm, which was further confirmed by breakthrough experiments showing that Cu-TDPAT can effectively capture $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ from the $\mathbf{CO}_{2}–\mathbf{N}_{2}$ mixed gas and can be fully regenerated under relatively mild conditions. In addition, Cu-TDPAT also shows extremely high gas uptake capacity under high pressure: total $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ of $6.77\%$ at $77~\mathrm{K}$ and 67 bar, total $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ of $\scriptstyle181\ {\mathrm{cm}}^{3}$ (STP) $\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ and 35 bar, excess $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ of $310~\mathrm{cm}^{3}$ (STP) $\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ and 48 bar. The same MOF was also synthesized by Eddaoudi et al. and named rht-MOF-7.144 But the conflict adsorption data were reported. For example, the $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ uptake of rht-MOF-7 at $273\mathrm{~K~}$ and 1 bar is $146~\mathrm{cm}^{3}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , while that for Cu-TDPAT is $227\ \mathrm{cm}^{3}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , which may be due to the degree of sample activation.
Recently, Wang et al.145 synthesized a triazole-containing dendritic hexacarboxylate linker, $5,5^{\prime},5^{\prime\prime}–4,4^{\prime},4^{\prime\prime}.$ -(benzene-1,3,5- triyl)tris(1H-1,2,3-triazole-4,1-diyl)triisophthalic acid $\left(\mathbf{H}_{6}\mathbf{B}22\right)$ , via ‘‘click’’ chemistry, and subsequently used it to successfully construct a highly porous nitrogen-rich rht-based MOF NTU105 incorporating accessible coordination-free triazole units (Fig. 13a). Different from the more usual fcc-packing in the prototypical rht-based net, the overall structure of NTU-105 is based on body centered tetragonal (bct) packing of cuboctahedra in 3D space. Likewise, three types of cages were formed within NTU-105, namely, cub- ${\cdot}O_{\mathrm{h}}$ , $\mathrm{T}{\cdot}T_{\mathrm{d}}$ and $\mathrm{T}–O_{\mathrm{h}}$ , with inner sphere diameters of 12, 15, and $20\textup{\AA}$ , respectively. The three types of cages tessellate in the same way as in the rht-based network. Significantly, combination of the coordination-free triazole moieties and the accessible copper sites within the framework provides strong interactions with the gas molecules and thus achieves high gas adsorption capacities. The $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ uptake capability reaches $187~\mathrm{cm}^{3}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at $273\mathrm{~K~}$ and 1 atm (Fig. 13b), making NTU-105 a top MOF material for $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ uptake reported to date.37 The isosteric heat of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption of $35~\mathrm{kJ~mol}^{-1}$ at low-loading range is also quite high among the rht-based MOFs reported so far, just below the highest value of $42.2\ \mathrm{kJ\mol^{-1}}$ for Cu-TDPAT.143 Molecular simulation clearly revealed that not only open copper sites but also nitrogen-rich triazole units within the framework were the preferential interaction sites for $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ , responsible for the high and selective $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ uptake. In addition, the desolvated NTU-105a also exhibits an exceptionally high $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ uptake capacity up to $2.75\%$ at $77\mathrm{~K~}$ and 1 atm (Fig. 13b). To date, there are only a handful of MOFs with $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ uptakes exceeding $2.6\%$ under the same conditions.32
The same MOF was also reported by Hupp’s group146 and Schro¨der’s group147 independently, and named as NU-125 and NOTT-122, respectively. In their reports, the high-pressure adsorption properties were investigated in detail. Taking NU-125 as an example, the maximum excess $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ uptake is about $6.0\mathrm{wt\%}$ at $77\mathrm{K}$ , but only $1.0~\mathrm{wt\%}$ of absolute $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ uptake was observed at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ and 65 bar. NU-125 has an absolute $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ storage capacity of $228~\mathrm{cm}^{3}\left(\mathrm{STP}\right)\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ and 58 bar, corresponding to $86\%$ of the $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ stored in a compressed natural gas (CNG) tank at 248 bar. Taking 5.8 bar as a specific lower pressure limit and 58 bar as the upper limit, the deliverable capacity of $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ of NU-125 is $174~\mathrm{cm}^{3}$ (STP) $\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ . The $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ uptake at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ and 30 bar is about $25~\mathrm{mmol}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ .
Different from most hexatopic carboxylates containing three coplanar isophthalate units used to construct rht-based MOFs, as discussed above, Sun et al. synthesized a series of $C_{3}$ -symmetric hexacarboxylate ligands $\left(\mathrm{H}_{6}\mathrm{B}15\mathrm{-}\mathrm{H}_{6}\mathrm{B}17\right)$ with three isophthalate units being non-coplanar.148 Interestingly, their solvothermal assembly with dicopper paddlewheel $\mathrm{{Cu}_{2}(\mathrm{{COO})_{4}}}$ SBUs also resulted in the formation of three (3,24)-connected MOFs SDU-6-8 (SDU represents ‘‘Shandong University’’) with the same rht-based network as reported structures. Because the functional groups point toward the center of the $\mathbf{T}–O_{\mathrm{h}}$ cage, the cavity size of the $\mathrm{\DeltaT}{\cdot}O_{\mathrm{h}}$ cage decreases with the increase of the size of the inner functional groups. The cavity radius of the $\mathrm{{T}}{\cdot}O_{\mathrm{h}}$ cage changes from $6.0\mathring{\mathrm{~A~}}$ in SDU-6, $5.6\mathring{\mathrm{~A~}}$ in SDU-7 to $4.5\mathrm{~\AA~}$ in SDU-8, respectively. However, the cub- ${\cdot}O_{\mathrm{h}}$ and T- $\cdot T_{\mathrm{d}}$ cages are fixed in SDU-6-8 with the approximate cavity radius of 6.6 and $6.7\mathring{\mathbf{A}}$ respectively. Thus, this series of MOFs provides a great chance to systematically investigate the effect of the functional groups on the gas adsorption capacity. The desolvated materials SDU-6-8 take up 2.15, 2.13 and $2.03\ \mathrm{wt\%}$ $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ at $77~\mathrm{K}$ and 1 bar, respectively. The higher $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ uptake capacity of SDU-6 compared to the other two is attributed to its large surface area as well as the stronger polar hydroxyl group. In the low-pressure region (less than 0.27 bar), the $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ uptake of SDU-8 is slightly larger than SDU-7, which is due to the fact that the bulky functional group decreases the pore size and enhances the roughness of the pore surface. Due to the highest surface area of SDU-6, it has the highest maximum excess $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ uptake capacity of $5.73~\mathrm{wt\%}$ . The total gravimetric $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ uptakes of SDU-6-8 are 7.63, 7.16 and $6.45~\mathrm{wt\%}$ at $77~\mathrm{K}$ and 55 bar, respectively. At $298\mathrm{~K~}$ and 35 bar, the total volumetric $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ uptake capacities of SDU-6-8 are 172, 160 and $\mathrm{147~cm^{3}}$ (STP) $\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ , respectively. This study indicates that the polarity of the functional groups has a significant effect on the gas adsorption of an MOF, and decoration of the cage walls with strong polar groups can enhance the adsorption capacities for $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ , $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ .
A copper–hexacarboxylate framework UTSA-20 was constructed using a hexacarboxylic acid, $3,3^{\prime},3^{\prime\prime},5,5^{\prime},5^{\prime\prime}$ -benzene1,3,5-triyl-hexabenzoic acid $\left(\mathbf{H}_{6}\mathbf{b}\mathbf{h}\mathbf{b},\ \mathbf{H}_{6}\mathbf{B}1\right)$ , exhibiting a novel topology of zyg instead of rht-related. This is because there exist steric hindrance and repulsion between the two closest axial ligands in the dicopper paddlewheel units in the two closest cuboctahedra if the suppositional rht-related network is formed. Two different types of 1D channels are formed within the framework with the potential open copper sites exposed to the pore surfaces: rectangular pores with dimensions of $3.4\times4.8\mathring{\mathbf{A}}$ and cylindrical pores with a diameter of $8.5\mathrm{~\AA~}$ (Fig. 14a). The framework exhibits the absolute volumetric $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ storage capacity of $195~\mathrm{cm}^{3}$ (STP) $\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ at $300\mathrm{~K~}$ and 35 bar (Fig. 14b).149 Computational investigations indicate that besides the open copper sites, the linker channel sites are also one of the primary $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ adsorption sites, and that the $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ binding at the linker channel sites is even stronger than that at OMSs because the $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ molecule is well sandwiched between two bhb linker potential surfaces. The combination of the two adsorption sites (open copper sites and linker channel sites) contributes to $\sim90\%$ of the experimental uptake at $300\mathrm{~K~}$ and 35 bar. The remaining $\sim10\%$ may be easily attributed to additional secondary binding sites which exhibit lower heats of adsorption. The $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ storage density of $0.222\ \mathrm{g}\ \mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ in micropores in UTSA-20 at $300\mathrm{~K~}$ and 35 bar is equivalent to the density of compressed $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ at $300~\mathrm{K}$ and 340 bar. Such high $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ storage density is attributed to the full utilization of both open copper sites and the pore space.
## 3.2 Other metal tri-isophthalate frameworks
Solvothermal assembly of $\mathrm{H}_{6}\mathrm{B}18$ with zinc(II) ions generated a rht-based MOF.150 However, the sample lost its crystallinity and turned into a non-porous material after removal of the guest molecules, apparently due to the intrinsic instability of dizinc paddlewheel $\mathrm{Zn}_{2}\mathrm{(COO)}_{4}$ SBUs and the non-rigid ligand. In fact, a similar MOF PCN-60 constructed from the zinc paddlewheel SBUs and $\mathbf{H}_{6}\mathbf{B}3$ ligands also suffers from such instability upon the removal of guest molecules.136 So, rigid ligands and stable paddlewheel clusters are necessary to stabilize the (3,24)- connected rht-based MOFs.
The same ligand $\mathbf{H}_{6}\mathbf{B}18$ was also used by Bai et al. to synthesize a lanthanide MOF (Ln-MOF) NJU-Bai11 $\mathrm{\big[Y_{2}\big(B18\big)\big(H_{2}O\big)_{2}\big],}$ 151 crystallizing in a chiral space group $P2_{1}2_{1}2_{1}$ . Structurally, the carboxylate groups from B18 ligands bridge the ${\bf Y}^{3+}$ ions into a helical chain, which is further linked to eight neighboring helices by the B18 ligand to form a 3D network. There are open channels with a diameter of about $8\mathring{\mathbf{A}}$ along the $a$ direction. The activated NJU-Bai11a possesses a BET surface area of
$1152~\mathrm{~m~}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , comparable to the largest surface areas ever reported in Ln-MOFs.152–154 Remarkably, NJU-Bai11a exhibits excellent adsorption capacity for $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ $\mathrm{130~cm^{3}~g^{-1}}$ at $273\mathrm{~K~}$ and 1 bar), and $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ ( $1.43~\mathrm{wt\%}$ at $77~\mathrm{K}$ and 1 bar). The $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ adsorption enthalpy of $7.05\mathrm{kJ}\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ is close to the highest value of $7.3\mathrm{kJ}\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ reported among Ln-MOFs.155 The high enthalpy of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption of $30.3\mathrm{\kJ\mol^{-1}}$ at zero coverage as well as the good separation selectivities of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ over $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and ${\bf N}_{2}$ clearly indicated the preferable affinity of the inserted amide groups in the structure towards $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ than $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ , since no molecule sieving effect exists in NJU-Bai11 because the size of the open channel $(8\ \mathring{\mathbf{A}})$ along the $a$ direction is much bigger than that of gas molecules such as $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ (3.3 Å), $\mathrm{CH}_{4}\left(3.8\mathring{\mathrm{A}}\right)$ , and $\tilde{\mathbf{N}}_{2}\left(3.64\mathring{\mathbf{A}}\right)$ .
Recently, Shi et al. used the hexacarboxylate ligand $\mathbf{H}_{6}\mathbf{B}21$ to construct a luminescent MOF Zn-TDPAT $\left[\mathrm{Zn}_{3}(\mathbf{B}21)(\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O})_{3}\right]$ via a solvothermal reaction with $\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ .156 The X-ray single crystal diffraction results reveal that Zn-TDPAT has the same (3,24)-connected rht-based network as Cu-TDPAT (Fig. 15a). Most remarkably, this compound exhibited the significant luminescence quenching effect for nitrobenzene (Fig. 15b), and the luminescence intensities decrease monotonically with an increase in the concentrations of nitrobenzene (Fig. 15c). Such luminescence quenching is ascribed to the electron transfer from the benzene rings of the TDPAT ligand to electrondeficient nitrobenzene. The luminescence of Zn-TDPAT can be easily recovered by simply filtrating and washing several times with DMF. In addition, the luminescence intensity of Zn-TDPAT is enhanced linearly with decreasing temperature because cooling is gradually limiting the thermally activated intramolecular rotations and non-radiative decay (Fig. 15d). Thus, this compound can serve as a dual functional luminescent sensor for quantitatively detecting the concentration of nitrobenzene and temperature.
Two 3D MOFs, $\bf{Z n_{4}O(B1)}$ and $\mathbf{Zn}_{3}(\mathbf{B}1)$ , are assembled from the hexacarboxylate ligand $\mathbf{H}_{6}\mathbf{B}1$ with $\mathrm{Zn(n)}$ ions under different solvothermal conditions.157 The MOF $\bf{Z n_{4}O(B1)}$ was composed of $\mathrm{Zn_{4}O(C O O)_{6}}$ clusters linked by B1 ligands to form a 3D framework with NaCl-type topology, while the MOF $\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{n}_{3}(\mathbf{B}1)$ contains non-centrally symmetrical dinuclear SBUs which are connected by B1 ligands to generate a 3D network with a different cor based topology. Treatment of $\mathbf{H}_{6}\mathbf{B}\mathbf{1}$ ligands with $\mathbf{Cd(NO_{3})_{2}}{\cdot}4\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ afforded another MOF $\mathbf{Cd}_{3}(\mathbf{B}1)$ . Compared with the MOF $\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{n}_{3}(\mathbf{B}1)$ , this Cd-based MOF contains two types of dinuclear SBUs which are further connected by B1 ligands to form a 3D network of the same cor-based topology. The activated MOF $\mathrm{{Zn_{4}O(B1)}}$ exhibits a BET surface area of $156\mathrm{~m}^{2}\mathrm{~g}^{-1}$ , and takes up $0.38~\mathrm{wt\%}$ of $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ at $77~\mathrm{K}$ and 1 atm, and $35.0~\mathrm{cm}^{3}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ and $10.8~\mathrm{cm}^{3}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ of $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ at $273\mathrm{~K~}$ and 1 bar. However, the activated MOF $\mathbf{Cd}_{3}(\mathbf{L1})$ did not take up any gas molecule, but adsorbed methanol of $6.5\%$ at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ and 160 mbar, indicating that the activated sample might be partially recovered back into the original porous structure.
Sun et al. synthesized a trimethyl substituted bhb ligand $\mathrm{\cdot\mathrm{H_{6}t m b h b}}$ , $\mathbf{H}_{6}\mathbf{B}2\dot{\mathbf{\Phi}}$ ) and constructed the corresponding zinc-based MOF SDU-1.158 Due to the introduction of the sterically congested methyl group on the linker, the carboxylate groups rotate to form a large dihedral angle with respect to the central phenyl ring, compared to those in the above two zinc based MOFs, thus resulting in the formation of a different network. This framework contains two kinds of SBUs, namely, $[\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{COO})_{3}]$ and $[\mathrm{Zn_{2}(C O O)_{4}}]$ . Six $[\mathrm{Zn_{2}(C O O)_{4}}]$ SBUs are linked together by 8 tmbhb ligands to form a trisoctahedron (Fig. 16a). Four $[\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{COO})_{3}]$ SBUs connect with 4 tmbhb ligands to form another cubic polyhedron (Fig. 16b). Two different kinds of polyhedra are packed into a complicated 3D framework. Topologically, the framework can be simplified in three different manners. If one takes $[\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{COO})_{3}]$ SBU and trisoctahedral SBB as 3- and 24-connected nodes, respectively, the resulting 3D framework is a (3,24) connected network with a rht topology. If $[\mathrm{Zn_{2}(C O O)_{4}}]$ SBU and cubic SBB are treated as 4- and 12-connected nodes, respectively, the framework is a (4,12)-connected ftw network. If two kinds of SBUs and tmbhb ligand are regarded as 3-, 4-, and 6-connected nodes, respectively, this framework can be simplified to a novel (3,4,6)-connected network.
## 4. Multifunctional MOFs based on tetra-isophthalates
Scheme 4 summarizes a variety of octacarboxylate linkers containing four isophthalate moieties at the end, which have been incorporated into porous MOFs.
## 4.1 Copper tetra-isophthalate frameworks
Lin et al. synthesized a series of chiral binaphthyl-based aromatics-rich octacarboxylic acids $\left(\mathrm{H}_{8}\mathrm{C}1-\mathrm{H}_{8}\mathrm{C}3\right)$ and used them to construct an isoreticular family of highly porous and robust MOFs, which were obtained by treating the corresponding organic linkers with $\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}2.5\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ under solvothermal conditions.159 Structurally, the organic linker is linked to 8 dicopper paddlewheels via carboxylate groups to form a scu-based net where the dicopper paddlewheel and the ligand act as 4- and 8-conencted nodes, respectively (Fig. 17a and b). As expected, the open channel sizes along the (001) and (110) direction decrease with the increasing steric bulk of hydroxyl, ethoxyl and benzyloxy groups on the $^{2,2^{\prime}}$ -position of the binaphthyl moieties of the octacarboxylic acid bridging ligands (Fig. 17c–h). Notably, the high connectivity of the ligands has dramatically enhanced the framework stability against removal of guest molecules. The activated samples $[\mathrm{Cu}_{4}\mathrm{C}1],$ $\left[\mathrm{Cu}_{4}\mathrm{C}2\right]$ and $\left[\mathrm{Cu}_{4}\mathrm{C}3\right]$ exhibit the high BET surface areas of 2149, 2285 and $1605~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , respectively, and take up a large amount of $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ of $2.5\mathrm{wt\%}$ , $2.1\mathrm{wt\%}$ and $1.8~\mathrm{wt\%}$ at 77 K and 1 bar, respectively. Of three compounds, the compound $\left[\mathrm{Cu}_{4}\mathrm{C}3\right]$ exhibited the lowest $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ uptake of $1.8~\mathrm{wt\%}$ due to its smallest surface area and pore volume. Although the compound $\left[\mathrm{Cu}_{4}\mathrm{C}2\right]$ has a higher surface area than the compound $\mathrm{[Cu_{4}C1]}$ , it exhibits a much lower $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ uptake of $2.1\mathrm{wt\%}$ presumably due to its larger pore size relative to the compound $\mathsf{[C u_{4}C1]}$ . This study highlights the bright promise of using bridging ligands of high connectivity to build highly stable and porous MOFs for gas uptake applications.
Expanding this strategy, the same group synthesized a series of (4,8)-connected MOFs using the elongated chiral binaphthylderived aromatic-rich octacarboxylate ligands $\left(\mathrm{H}_{8}\mathrm{C}4-\mathrm{H}_{8}\mathrm{C}6\right)$ .160 The MOF $\mathrm{([cu_{4}C5])}$ crystallizes in orthorhombic $C222$ space group, while other two MOFs $\mathrm{([Cu_{4}C4]}$ and $\mathrm{[Cu_{4}C6])}$ crystallize in the tetragonal $P4$ space group, but they have very similar structural characteristics. Because of the elongated ligands, the three MOFs possess very large solvent accessible volumes. After freeze-drying treatment, the MOFs $\left[\mathrm{Cu_{4}C4}\right]$ , $\left[\mathrm{Cu}_{4}\mathrm{C}5\right]$ and $\mathrm{[Cu_{4}C6]}$ exhibit BET surface areas of 1942, 2448, and $1189~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , respectively, which are however significantly lower than those calculated from single-crystal structures, indicating that the MOFs have undergone some framework distortion upon desolvation, which has also been confirmed by PXRD studies showing that many of the PXRD peaks of the evacuated samples have disappeared. The three MOFs take up a moderate amount of $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ of $1.7\mathrm{wt\%}$ , $1.8\mathrm{wt\%}$ and $1.4\mathrm{wt\%}$ at $77\mathrm{K}$ and 1 atm, respectively. The studies indicate that the strategy of the high network connectivity is an effective route to stabilize MOFs with highly porous structures but some framework distortion still occurs when the bridging ligands become much longer.
Schro¨der et al. synthesized a tetrahedrally branched octacarboxylate ligand $\mathrm{(H_{8}C8)}$ and prepared a robust and highly porous metal–organic polyhedral framework NOTT-140 based on the scu net, which consists of four-connected $\mathrm{{Cu}_{2}(\mathrm{{COO})_{4}}}$ paddlewheels bridged through eight-branched C8 linkers.161 The framework structure can be viewed as the alternate packing of two types of polyhedral cages A and B in 3D space. As shown in Fig. 18, cage A $\mathbf{\widetilde{5.5\AA}}$ in diameter) is an octahedron composed of 4 dicopper paddlewheels and 4 isophthalate units from two ligands. Cage B (8.9 Å in diameter) is a slightly distorted cuboctahedron composed of 8 dicopper paddlewheels and 4 isophthalate units. Each cage A is surrounded by 8 cage B units via the sharing of a triangular face and vice versa. The desolvated NOTT-140 exhibits a high BET surface area of $2620~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , which is much larger than the values for other reported porous (4,8)-connected MOF materials.159 NOTT-140 adsorbs $2.5~\mathrm{wt\%}$ of $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ at $77\mathrm{~K~}$ and 1 bar. Such a high uptake at low pressure can be attributed to the high affinity of $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ for the open copper sites and the presence of the small octahedral cage A. The total $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ uptake of NOTT-140 reaches $6.0\mathrm{wt\%}$ at $77\mathrm{K}$ and 20 bar, which is moderate compared with the highest capacity MOF materials. The isosteric heat of $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ adsorption at zero coverage is $4.15~\mathrm{kJ~mol^{-1}}$ , which is relatively low. The main reason is that the open copper sites in NOTT-140a are outside the cages, thus reducing the affinity between the framework and $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ molecules. At $293\mathrm{~K~}$ and 20 bar, $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ uptakes of NOTT-140a are $\mathrm{145\cm^{3}}$ (STP) $\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ and 19.53 mmol $\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , respectively.
A microporous MOF PCN-922 consisting of a dendritic octatopic organic linker $\mathrm{{{H}_{8}}}$ ettb $\mathrm{(H_{8}C9)}$ and dicopper paddlewheel SBUs, which could not be assembled directly from copper(II) salts and ${\mathrm{H}}_{8}{\mathrm{ettb}}$ , has been successfully synthesized by post-synthetic metathesis using a dizinc paddlewheel based MOF PCN-921 as a template.162 PCN-921 was easily obtained via a solvothermal reaction between $\mathrm{{{H}_{8}}}$ ettb and $\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ in DEF in the presence of $\mathrm{HBF_{4}}$ . Upon immersing PCN-921 in a DMF solution of $\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}$ at RT for 4 days, the zinc ions in PCN-921 could be completely replaced by copper ions, which was confirmed by X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) and single-crystal XRD studies. In the structure of the resultant Cu-based MOF PCN-922, each ettb ligand connects 8 dicopper paddlewheel SBUs in a rectangular prismatic arrangement, forming a highly porous (4,8)-connected 3D network of scu topology. The framework contains two types of microporous cages with different sizes. The small cage with the diameter of $14\mathring{\textbf{A}}$ is composed of 8 dicopper paddlewheel SBUs and 8 ettb linkers (Fig. 19a), while the large one with the diameter of $18\mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ is formed by 8 dicopper paddlewheel SBUs and 12 ettb linkers (Fig. 19b). The two types of cages are arranged in an alternate fashion, forming 1D channels of $9\mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ running along the $c$ axis of the framework (Fig. 19c). After desolvation, PCN-921a hardly adsorbed any ${\bf N}_{2}$ gas, while PCN-922a exhibited a typical type-I $\ensuremath{\mathbf{N}}_{2}$ sorption isotherm, revealing the microporous nature of the framework with a BET surface area of $2006\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ . Additionally, PCN-922a has very high $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ and $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ uptake capacities under low pressure: $2.3~\mathrm{wt\%}$ of $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ at 77 K and 1 bar, and $81\mathrm{cm}^{3}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ and 1 bar.
Du et al. synthesized a tetrapodal octadentate ligand, ${}5,{5}^{\prime},{5}^{\prime\prime},{5}^{\prime\prime\prime}.$ - silanetetrayltetraisophthalic acid $\left(\mathrm{H}_{8}\mathrm{C}11\right)$ , which is assembled with dicopper paddlewheels under solvothermal reaction conditions to generate a 3D porous (4,8)-connected MOF of scu based topology.163 There exist open channels of $4.7\times6.3\mathrm{~}\mathring{\mathbf{A}}^{2}$ , $4.7\ \times6.3\ \mathring{\mathrm{A}}^{2}$ and $7.2\ \times\ 7.2\ \mathring{\mathrm{A}}^{2}$ along the $a,\ b$ and $c$ axes, respectively. The guest solvent molecules can be only partially removed even by using the SCD method. The ${\bf N}_{2}$ adsorption isotherm reveals a type-I behavior with a BET surface area of $875~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ . The $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ uptake capacity is $1.88~\mathrm{wt\%}$ at $77~\mathrm{K}$ and 1 atm, which is moderately high. Additionally, the compound takes up different amounts of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ ( $\cdot130\mathrm{cm}^{3}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ and $21\mathrm{cm}^{3}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ of $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ at $273\mathrm{~K~}$ and 1 atm), as a result of their different kinetic diameters and quadrupole moments.
An octacarboxylate organic linker with condensed $m$ -benzenedicarboxylate moieties, 1,2,4,5-tetra(5-isophthalic acid)benzene $\left(\mathrm{H}_{8}\mathrm{C}12\right)$ , and its corresponding copper-based MOF UTSA-34 were synthesized by our research group.164 UTSA-34 is a 3D framework in which 6 carboxylate groups of the ligand are connected to the dicopper paddlewheel SBUs. There exist large interconnected cages of about $12.8\mathrm{~\AA~}$ in diameter that are surrounded by 24 isophthalates moieties. The pore structure can be regarded as a fcc close packing of spherical cages, each connected to 12 nearest neighboring cages. The desolvated UTSA-34 exhibits the promising potential for highly selective separation of $C_{2}$ hydrocarbons from $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ , which was comprehensively examined by IAST and breakthrough calculations. UTSA-34 possesses higher separation capacity and selectivity than UTSA- $33^{165}$ and the well investigated ZIF-8 (ZIF represents ‘‘zeolitic imidazolate framework’’).166
A robust MOF PCN-26 constructed from the flexible octacarboxylate ligand, tetrakis[(3,5-dicarboxyphenoxy) methyl]- methane $\left(\mathrm{H}_{8}\mathrm{C}10\right)$ , and dicopper paddlewheel $\mathrm{{Cu}}_{2}(\mathrm{{COO})_{4}}$ SBUs was reported by Zhou et al. Solvothermal reaction of $\mathbf{H}_{8}\mathbf{C}10$ with $\mathrm{CuBr}_{2}$ in DMF afforded this MOF.167 In the structure, PCN-26 contains two different kinds of cages. One is an octahedral cage in which four dicopper paddlewheel SBUs and two quaternary carbon atoms of two ligands occupy the vertices, and four $\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{O}\cdot$ –isophthalate moieties of the ligands occupy four faces (Fig. 20a). The other one is a cuboctahedral cage where eight dicopper paddlewheels and four quaternary carbon atoms occupy the 12 vertices (Fig. 20b). Every cuboctahedron is surrounded by 8 neighboring octahedra to form a polyhedronstacking 3D framework with open channels of $7.57\ \times\ 7.57$ , $8.13~\times~8.13$ , and $7.93\ \times\ 7.93\ \mathring{\mathrm{A}}^{2}$ along the $a,\ b$ and $c$ axes, respectively (Fig. 20c). Remarkably, the resulting MOF is rigid, although the flexible ligand is used. The ${\bf N}_{2}$ sorption isotherm revealed that the desolvated PCN-26 exhibited type-I sorption behavior with a BET surface area of $1733\ \mathrm{~m}^{2}\ \mathrm{~g}^{-1}$ . PCN-26 adsorbs $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ of $2.57~\mathrm{wt\%}$ at $77~\mathrm{K}$ and 1 atm, rising to $2.86~\mathrm{wt\%}$ at $77~\mathrm{K}$ and 32.4 bar. Also, PCN-26 selectively adsorbs $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ over $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and ${\bf N}_{2}$ at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ with the selectivities of 6.3 for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and 21 for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ . The same MOF was also reported by Du et al.168 Due to the different activation methods, a lower BET surface area of $1115~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ and $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ uptake of $2.12~\mathrm{wt\%}$ at $77\mathrm{~K~}$ and 1 bar were observed. The $\mathbf{C}_{2}\mathbf{H}_{2}$ adsorption was also investigated. This MOF exhibits a high $\mathbf{C}_{2}\mathbf{H}_{2}$ uptake of $154~\mathrm{cm}^{3}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at $296~\mathrm{~K~}$ and 1 atm, comparable to that of MOF- $\mathbf{505}^{72}$ and $\mathbf{Cu}_{2}(\mathbf{EBTC})^{71}$ despite a low surface area.
Eddaoudi et al. used the octacarboxylate ligands $\left(\mathrm{H_{8}C14,H_{8}C15}.\right.$ and $\mathbf{H_{8}C16}$ , Scheme 4) to construct a family of isoreticular MOFs.169
Solvothermal reaction of $5,5^{\prime},5^{\prime\prime},5^{\prime\prime\prime}$ -[1,2,4,5-phenyltetramethoxy]- tetraisophthalate $\left(\mathrm{{H_{8}C14}}\right)$ with $\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}2.5\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ yielded a crystalline material Cu-C14. The in situ formed dicopper paddlewheel units are bridged by 1,3-benzenedicarboxylate to form 2D Cu-(5-R-isophthalate) supermolecular building layers (SBLs), which are pillared via the 4-connected ligand cores to give rise to a 3D network of tbo topology, analogous to the prototypical HKUST-1.64 There exist three kinds of polyhedral cages in the framework, namely, truncated tetrahedron, truncated cube and truncated cuboctahedron, shown in Fig. 21. The largest truncated cuboctahedra are surrounded by 6 truncated cubes and 8 truncated tetrahedra. The truncated cubes are surrounded by 6 truncated cuboctahedra and 8 truncated tetrahedra. The truncated tetrahedra are surrounded by 4 truncated tetrahedra and 4 truncated cubes. When the ligand $\mathbf{H}_{6}\mathbf{C}15$ is used instead, the MOF Cu-C15 with the same tbo topology was formed in which the two added pendant arms are not metal-coordinated, and point into the largest polyhedral cavities. As illustrated in MOF Cu-C16, the ligand extension expands the size of all the cages. Such an isoreticular tbo-derived MOFs series provides an excellent platform to evaluate the impact of pore size, shape and functionality on the gas sorption. Thus, the authors compared the gas sorption properties of MOFs Cu-C14 and Cu-C15. As expected, the Langmuir apparent surface area of $1490~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ for $\mathbf{Cu-C15}$ is smaller than that of $2896~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ for Cu-C14. The presence of the uncoordinated carboxylate groups in the framework Cu-C15 leads to high adsorption heat over the entire studied range but negatively affects the gas uptake at high pressure.
## 4.2 Other metal tetra-isophthalate frameworks
Du et al.170 prepared a photoluminscent microporous anionic MOF $[(\mathrm{Me}_{2}\mathrm{NH}_{2})_{2}\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{C}10]$ with a (4,8)-connected scu topology by solvothermal self-assembly of $\mathrm{In}\big(\mathrm{NO}_{3}\big)_{3}{\cdot}4.5\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and $\mathbf{H}_{8}\mathbf{C}10$ in DMF. There are two crystallographically independent $\operatorname{In}(\operatorname{In})$ ions in the asymmetric unit cell: one $\operatorname{In}\left(\operatorname{III}\right)$ ion is six-coordinated by four oxygen atoms from two chelating carboxylate groups and two oxygen atoms from two monodentate carboxylate groups, while the other $\operatorname{In}\left(\operatorname{InI}\right)$ ion is eight-coordinated by eight oxygen atoms from four chelating carboxylate groups, but both $\operatorname{In}(\operatorname{III})$ ions serve as 4-connected nodes, which are bridged by the octacarboxylate to form an anionic open framework encapsulating charge balancing $\mathrm{{\bfMe}}_{2}{\mathrm{NH}_{2}}^{+}$ counterions and DMF and $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ guest molecules. Alternatively, the structure can be viewed as the alternate packing of two types of cages (Fig. 22c). The small cage is an octahedral cage with the diameter of $4.2\mathring{\mathrm{~A~}}$ composed of $4~\mathrm{{In}(\mathrm{{III})}}$ ions and 4 isophthalate units from two ligands (Fig. 22a). The large one is a slightly distorted cuboctahedral cage with the diameter of $8.8\mathring{\mathrm{~A~}}$ composed of $8~\mathrm{{In}(\mathrm{{III})}}$ ions and 4 isophthalate units (Fig. 22b). The permanent porosity was established by ${\bf N}_{2}$ sorption isotherm, exhibiting a BET surface area of $752~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ . The desolvated MOF takes up $1.11~\mathrm{wt\%}$ of $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ at $77~\mathrm{K}$ and 1 atm, and selectively adsorbs $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ over $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ . In addition, this MOF exhibited a strong fluorescent emission peak at ${360}\mathrm{nm}$ upon excitation at $280\ \mathrm{nm}$ at RT, which is blue shifted compared to the free ligand. More interestingly, the emission intensities of this MOF varied upon contact with different solvents. Aromatic guest molecules with electrondonating groups such as toluene and $p$ -xylene enhanced the emission intensity, while aromatic guest molecules with electronwithdrawing groups such as nitrobenzene, 1,3-dinitrobenzene and 2,4-dinitrotoluene significantly quenched the emission. Furthermore, this MOF is more sensitive towards nitrobenzene than 1,3-dinitrobenzene and 2,4-dinitrotoluene, which is due to the pore sieving effect allowing nitrobenzene to pass through but blocking 1,3-dinitrobenzene and 2,4-dinitrotoluene. The excellent fluorescence quenching response to nitroaromatics can be attributed to the electrostatic interactions between the anionic framework and the electron-deficient nitroaromatic analytes. Thus, this MOF can be regarded as an efficient porous material based sensor for detecting nitroaromatic explosives.
The same MOF was also synthesized by Cao’s group.171 When the sample was activated using the SCD method, a larger BET surface area of $1555~\mathrm{~m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ and higher gas sorption capacities $(1.49~\mathrm{wt\%}$ of $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ at $77~\mathrm{K}$ and 1 bar, $56.2~\mathrm{cm}^{3}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ and $13.2\thinspace{\mathrm{cm}}^{3}{\mathrm{g}}^{-1}$ of $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ at $298\mathrm{K}$ and 1 bar) were obtained, once again highlighting the significance of activation methods on controlling the porosities.
Replacing DMF with DMA in the starting materials afforded another supramolecular isomer.172 In the asymmetric unit cell there exists only one crystallographically independent $\operatorname{In}\left(\operatorname{InI}\right)$ ion, which is coordinated by four chelating carboxylate groups to generate a mononuclear 4-connected $[\mathrm{In}(\mathrm{COO})_{4}]$ node that is further linked by the octacarboxylate ligand to form a 3D (4,8)-connected anionic framework. There were two types of channels in the structure: one elliptical channel with a diameter of about $8.8\times5.7\mathring{\mathbf{A}}^{2}$ along the $a$ or $b$ axes, and another rectangular one with a diameter of about $5.8\ \times\ 5.7\ \mathring{\mathbf{A}}^{2}$ along the $\boldsymbol{c}$ axis. The counterions $\mathrm{Me}_{2}\mathrm{NH_{2}}^{+}$ can be completely postsynthetically exchanged with $\mathrm{TMA}^{+}$ (tetramethylammonium), $\mathrm{TEA}^{+}$ (tetraethylammonium) and $\mathrm{TPA}^{+}$ (tetrapropylammonium), while only half of the $\mathrm{{\bfMe}}_{2}{\mathrm{{NH}}_{2}}^{+}$ cations were replaced by $\mathrm{TBA}^{+}$ (tetrabutylammonium) even on prolonging the reaction time and increasing the concentration of $\mathrm{TBA}^{+}$ . Remarkably, the resultant MOFs, labelled as A, B, C, D, exhibited distinctly different stabilities and gas sorption properties. For example, upon desolvation, the compound A loses its crystallinity while compounds B–D remain intact. The compound B shows typical type-I behavior with a BET surface area of $756\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ . In contrast, no significant ${\bf N}_{2}$ adsorption was observed for the compound C, probably due to the $\mathrm{TPA}^{+}$ ions blocking the channels. Moreover, the compound D only adsorbs a small amount of ${\bf N}_{2}$ , indicating that only part of the channels were blocked by $\mathrm{TBA}^{+}$ due to the partial exchange. Of particular interest is the fact that the compound C exhibits selective adsorption of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ and $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ but no $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ , indicative of its potential applications in both $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ enrichment and $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ capture. This study clearly indicates that gas sorption properties of the framework can be modulated by a judicious choice of the guest cations.
Two isostructural porphyrin-based MOFs (MMPF-4 and MMPF-5) were synthesized by Ma et al.173 using a custom-designed porphyrin, tetrakis(3,5-dicarboxyphenyl)porphine ( $\mathrm{{\tilde{H}}_{10}}$ tdcpp, $\mathbf{H_{8}C13}\mathrm{\dot{}}$ ). Solvothermal reaction of the $\mathrm{H_{10}t d c p p}$ ligand with $\mathrm{Zn}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}$ and $\mathrm{Cd}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}$ , respectively, in DMSO at $408\mathrm{~K~}$ afforded single crystals of MMPF-4 and MMPF-5. In MMPF-4, eight $[\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{COO})_{3}]$ paddlewheels are linked by 6 in situ metallated porphyrin moieties Zn-tdcpp to form a small cubicuboctahedron serving as a SBB (Fig. 23a), which is connected with 6 adjacent SBBs through 6 Zn-tdcpp moieties to generate an augmented pcu network where each SBB is considered as a 6-connected node (Fig. 23b).
In the assembly, another type of octahemioctahedral cage was also formed between the SBBs (Fig. 23c). Similarly, MMPF-5 is also based upon a small cubicuboctahedron SBB composed of six Cd-tdcpp moieties that are linked by 8 $[\mathrm{Cd}(\mathrm{COO})_{3}]$ moieties. Remarkably, MMPF-4 exhibited high $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ uptake capacity and excellent $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ adsorption selectivity. $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption capacity at 1 atm reaches up to $124{\mathrm{~cm}}^{3}{\mathrm{~g}}^{-1}$ at $273\mathrm{~K~}$ and $67~\mathrm{cm}^{3}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at $298\ \mathrm{K}$ . The IAST adsorption selectivity of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ over $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ is as high as 123 for a $15:85\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ gas mixture at $273\mathrm{~K~}$ and 1 bar.
In the crystal structure of MMPF-5, the $\operatorname{Cd}(\operatorname{II})$ cation residing within the porphyrin ring of the tdcpp ligand lies far out of the porphyrin plane, indicating the $\operatorname{Cd}(\operatorname{II})$ cations within the porphyrin macrocycle are readily substituted by other metal ions. Thus, upon immersing the crystals of MMPF-5 in the DMSO solution of $\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ at $358~\mathrm{~K~}$ for two days,174 the catalytically inactive Cd(II) based MMPF-5 was transformed into a catalytically active MOF MMPF-5(Co). Single-crystal X-ray diffraction studies revealed that all of the $\operatorname{Cd}(\operatorname{II})$ cations within the porphyrin macrocycles of tdcpp ligands are exclusively replaced by the $\operatorname{Co}(\operatorname{II})$ cations but the $\operatorname{Cd}(\operatorname{II})$ cations serving as the nodes in the framework remain intact. The catalytic performance of MMPF- ${\bf5(C o)}$ was evaluated in epoxidation of trans-stilbene using TBHP as oxidant in acetonitrile at $333\ \mathrm{K}.$ Control experiments showed that MMPF-5(Co) outperformed the homogeneous counterpart (cobalt(II) metallated tetrakis(3,5- dicarboxymethylesterphenyl) porphine), and other $\operatorname{Co}(\operatorname{II})$ based MOFs examined such as MMPF- $2^{118}$ and PPF-1Co119 in terms of both yield and selectivity. Furthermore, MMPF- ${\bf5(C o)}$ could be used for five cycles without a significant drop in its catalytic activity.
Direct solvothermal reaction of the same ligand $\mathbf{H_{8}C13}$ with $\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ in DMA at $388\mathrm{~K~}$ afforded another robust MOF MMPF-2.118 In the structure, there are two types of the ligands featuring bridging carboxylate groups and chelated carboxylate groups, respectively. Three cobalt atoms were bridged by the $\upmu_{3}–\mathrm{OH}$ group and six carboxylate groups from six different tdcpp ligands to form a distorted trigonal prismatic SBU. Each SBU links six tdcpp ligands and every ligand connects with 8 SBUs, forming a (6,8,8)-connected 3D network. Derived from the Ar adsorption isotherm data, MMPF-2 has a Langmuir surface area of 2037 m2 g1, which is the highest among reported porphyrin-based MOFs. Also, MMPF-2 has a $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ uptake capacity of $101~\mathrm{cm}^{3}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ and 1 atm, which is also among the highest yet reported for porous MOFs under the same conditions.
Independently, Wu et al.175,176 incorporated octatopic metalloporphyrin ligands, metal 5,10,15,20-tetrakis(3,5-biscarboxylphenyl)- porphyrin $\mathbf{\left(M-H_{8}O C P P\right.}$ , $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{-}\mathbf{H_{8}C1}3$ , $\mathbf{M}=\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}(\mathrm{m})\mathbf{C}\mathbf{l}$ or $\mathrm{{Ni}(\mathrm{{II})}}$ , into three isostructural porous MOFs of tbo topology, namely, ZJU-18 $\mathrm{([Mn_{5}C l_{2}(M n C l-O C P P)(D M F)_{4}(H_{2}O)_{4}]\cdot2I}$ DMF $\mathrm{8CH_{3}C O O H\cdot14H_{2}O}\right)$ , ZJU-19 $\mathrm{(\mathrm{[Mn_{5}C l_{2}(N i{\cdot}O C P P)(H_{2}O)_{8}]{\cdot}\mathrm{7DMF{\cdot}6C H_{3}C O O H{\cdot}11H_{2}O)_{5}}}}$ , ZJU-20 $\mathrm{([Cd_{5}C l_{2}(M n C l-O C P P)(H_{2}O)_{6}]\cdot13D M F\cdot2C H_{3}C O O H\cdot9H_{2}O)_{4}}$ , and examined their catalytic activities for oxidation of alkylbenzenes. Of these three MPFs, ZJU-18 consisting of $\mathbf{Mn}\big(\mathrm{III}\big).$ - porphyrin units coordinated to manganese containing SBUs exhibited highly efficient and selective oxidation of alkylbenzene (Table 3). Furthermore, in terms of the catalytic activities, ZJU-18 is very superior to the molecular counterpart $\mathbf{Mn(m)Cl-Me_{8}O C P P}$ , indicating that such a porous metalloporphyrinic framework enhanced their catalytic activities by blocking the formation of the catalytically inactive dimetalloporphyrinic species.
A solvothermal reaction of $\mathrm{CdCl}_{2}{\cdot}2.5\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and an octacarboxylate ligand $\mathbf{H}_{8}\mathbf{C}7$ in DMF afforded a homochiral framework ocMOM-2 (ocMOMs represents ‘‘homochiral metal–organic materials that exhibit enantioselective organocatalysis’’).177 The $\operatorname{Cd}^{2+}$ ions are coordinated by four carboxylate groups from four different ligands to form a 4-connected node, which is further linked by 1,3-benzenedicarboxylate groups to yield a 2D layer containing triangles and pentagons. Ligand to ligand cross-linking of these sheets between the 5-positions of the 1,3-benezenedicarboxylate moieties afforded a 3D network with a new type of (4,8)-connected topology. Two types of channels were formed in the framework: $1.2\ \mathrm{nm}$ channels along the $a$ axis and $0.8\ \mathrm{nm}$ along the $b$ axis. The compound did not exhibit the permanent porosity even after activation using the SCD method. Since the permanent porosity is not the essential prerequisite for a heterogeneous catalysis in a solution, the compound might be useful for solution-based heterogeneous asymmetric catalysis applications.
## 5. Comparison of diverse topologies of the MOFs constructed from meta-benzenedicarboxylate units
## 5.1 Terminology, definitions, and preliminaries
In this section, we are concerned with some aspects of the underlying topology of the structures of the MOFs of this review. The nets of MOFs with polytopic linkers were recently reviewed178 and just some major points relevant to the topic of this review and some nets that illustrate these points are recalled here. For the convenience of the reader, we first review some basic definitions and terminologies. The topology is described by a net which is an abstract graph that is simple (edges are undirected and there are no loops or multiple edges between vertices) and connected (there is a path of edges linking every pair of vertices).179 We like to distinguish between the abstract net which consists of vertices and edges, and the embedding of the graph which consists of nodes (or branch points) and links. In an embedding, coordinates are assigned to vertices and we can talk about such things as the length of a link, or the coordination figure (e.g. square or tetrahedron) of a node (It is clearly meaningless to talk about the length of an edge of an abstract graph).
The nets we will be concerned with are crystallographic. This means that the combinatorial symmetry of the graph (its automorphism group) is isomorphic with a crystallographic space group which will be the maximum possible symmetry of an embedding in a crystal structure (excluding, of course, false apparent symmetry resulting from disorder in a crystal). Recognition of this simple fact would eliminate many wrong topology assignments.
A graph with one topological kind of vertex (or edge) is called vertex (or edge) transitive. More generally we define a transitivity $\textit{p q}$ that indicates that there are $p$ kinds of vertices and $q$ kinds of edges. Thus, in a maximum symmetry embedding of a net with transitivity $\textit{p q}$ , the vertices fall into $p$ groups with nodes in each group related by symmetry, but nodes in different groups not so related. Similarly there are $q$ groups of links.
A vertex that is common to $k$ edges is referred to as $k$ -coordinated (the term $k$ -connected has a different meaning in graph theory, so is avoided) and often written $k\mathbf{-c}.$ If the net has both $k-c$ and $j\mathrm{-c}$ vertices it is written as $(k,j)\cdot\mathtt{c}$ .
Some of the more important nets are collected in the RCSR database which, among other things, gives a maximum-symmetry embedding of the net.25 There they are assigned a symbol which may be three letters such as xyz or three letters plus an extension such as xyz-a. In particular the extension -a refers to an augmented net in which the nodes are replaced by their coordination figures.
Here we focus on the structures of MOFs with the tetratopic, hexatopic, and octatopic linkers of Schemes 1–4. These can be considered either as one single node (4-c, 6-c or 8-c) of the underlying net as is often done and is generally the case in the proceeding sections. For example, the first such MOF with an assigned topology was probably MOF-505 with 4-c dicopper paddlewheels joined by a tetracarboxylate linker.65 For that material the topology was reported as nbo and that is certainly correct. But it is now realized that one needs to go further to completely characterize the topology. As shown below, the linkers under discussion contain 3-c branch points (nodes), and it is best to describe the topology using the 3-c branch points as nodes rather than considering the linker as just one node.178 In fact even better is to do both, to identify a basic net with the linker as a single node and a derived net with the 3-c nodes explicitly included. Some reasons for this are: (i) previous practice has been inconsistent. Thus the tetratopic linkers are almost always considered as single 4-c nodes, but hexatopic linkers are almost never considered as single 6-c nodes. (ii) There are generally more than one derived nets corresponding to a given basic net. If the derived net is not identified, fundamentally different topologies cannot be distinguished, and indeed have been confused in the past. (iii) The maximum possible symmetry of the framework is that of the derived net. For example the derived net may be intrinsically chiral whereas the basic net is not. (iv) The organic SBU contains two (or in general maybe more) links that can be independently varied in length.
In the earlier review178 the Minimum Transitivity Principal was described. This states that ‘‘The underlying nets of MOFs and related materials tend to be nets of minimal transitivity’’. When linking two nodes the minimal edge transitivity is one and the basic nets of this type are now well documented.180 Now consider MOFs made with the linkers of Fig. 24. For the tetratopic linker in the derived net, there are at least two nodes (A and B) and two links (A–B and B–B); accordingly, the minimal transitivity is $\emph{p q}=2\ 2$ . For the hexatopic linker in the derived net, there are three kinds of nodes (A, B and C) and two kinds of links (A–B and B–C), so the minimal transitivity is 3 2. In the octatopic case there are again three kinds of nodes but for links there are two possibilities. Either [Fig. 24(c)] A–B and B–C or [Fig. 24(d)] A–B, B–C and C–C; accordingly the minimal transitivity will be 3 2 or 3 3, respectively. The majority of the MOFs in this review have minimal transitivity nets and the discussion in this section is mainly restricted to them.
## 5.2 MOFs with di-isophthalate tetratopic linkers
The most commonly observed topology has been found with essentially planar tetratopic linkers and the $\mathbf{Cu}_{2}$ paddlewheel SBUs. In most of these cases, the resulting topology is derived from the most-symmetrical net with planar 4-c nodes, namely nbo. However as explained above, the linker has two 3-c branch points and thus lower symmetry than that $\left(4/m m m\rightleftharpoons D_{4\mathrm{h}}\right)$ at the vertex sites in the nbo net. Accordingly there are many possible derived nets of lower symmetry. The two simplest, which account for most of the observed structures, are fof (the topology of MOF-505) and fog. The two nets have the same symmetry $\left(R\bar{3}m\right)$ and have generally not been distinguished in the literature. Of 25 MOFs of this type reported twenty have the fof topology and five the fog topology. A primitive cell for each of the structures is shown in Fig. 25. It is an interesting challenge for the theoretician to determine why one or the other of these two nets is found in real materials. Notice that the fog version has large open channels parallel to the rhombohedral $c$ axis. Both fog and fof have a minimal transitivity of 2 2.
A third (3,4)-c net, tfb, derived from nbo has also been found in tetracarboxylate MOFs with 4-c metal SBUs.115,116 The relation of the derived net to the basic net is illustrated in Fig. 26. This net again has a minimal transitivity of 2 2. As shown in the figure the structure contains cages surrounded by six 4-c linkers. These are arranged as in cubic closest packing (net fcu); hence the name fcu-MOF.116
## 5.3 MOFs with tri-isophthalate hexatopic linkers
By far, the largest numbers of MOFs with hexatopic linkers have the linker topology shown in Fig. 24 joining $\mathbf{Cu}_{2}$ paddlewheel SBUs. The underlying topology has been described in a number of ways including uninodal, binodal and trinodal nets. The most common approach, indicated above is to identify a tertiary building unit (TBU) of 12 linked paddlewheels in a cuboctahedral arrangement. This TBU is joined to $243\mathrm{-c}$ branch points to form the (3,24)-c net rht. This description was first given by
Eddaoudi et al. for a compound with a tritopic linker joining 3-c and 4-c metal SBUs and appropriate in that case as in fact the 3-c branch point.178 A similar description was also given for the same topology, now with a hexacarboxylate linker by Lah et al.150 It might be noted that the cuboctahedral TBU is the same as MOP-1 (Fig. 1) and is also illustrated in Fig. 29. As some authors have remarked, the pattern of metal SBUs (4-c nodes in the (3,4)-c net) alone is that of the uninodal 5-c net ubt.
Interestingly, no author has described the net as (4,6)-c with the linker considered as a single 6-c node. As remarked previously,179 that net would be noncrystallographic (have nonrigid body symmetries).
The ntt net requires essentially planar hexacarboxylate linkers. With a linker too small to allow planarity linking square paddlewheels, a different topology is found in UTSA-20.149 The linker has an overall trigonal prismatic shape and if the linker is considered a 6-c node the net is the basic net stp for linking square and trigonal prismatic SBUs. The (3,4)-c derived net taking the 3-c branch points of the linker as nodes is zyg. In this case, the TBU is an infinite column of paddlewheels as shown in Fig. 30 which might be compared with Fig. 29. zyg, like ntt, is a minimal transitivity net.
An interesting topology was found in JUC-100 (JUC represents ‘‘Jilin University China’’) in which a hexacarboxylate linker joined $\mathrm{{Zn}_{4}}$ SBUs with six octahedrally-arranged points of extension. Taking the linker as a 6-c node the net is that of NaCl (the binary version of the primitive cubic net pcu). However including the 3-c branch points of the linker as 3-c nodes one finds the derived net zxc. This is in fact just the positions of the atoms in calcite, $\mathbf{CaCO}_{3}$ with Ca bonded to six O, and C bonded to $^3\mathrm{~O~}$ (so O is bonded to $\mathbf{C}\mathbf{\t}2\mathbf{C}\mathbf{a})$ .178 Clearly in calcite we want to consider the three atoms explicitly, rather than as a NaCl packing of Ca and $\mathbf{CO}_{3}$ groups (although that is not wrong); likewise it is more informative to consider the three nodes of the net explicitly. This is again a minimal transitivity (3 2) net.
6. Comparison of the MOFs constructed from $m$ -benzenedicarboxylate units with others for their gas storage $(H_{2},C H_{4}$ and $C_{2}H_{2})$ and carbon dioxide capture
Table 4 summarizes crystal densities, pore volumes, BET surface areas and $\mathbf{C}_{2}\mathbf{H}_{2}$ adsorption of some porous MOFs constructed from the organic linkers containing the $m$ -benzenedicarboxylate unit. Among these MOFs, NOTT-109 performs the best in terms of gravimetric and volumetric $\mathbf{C}_{2}\mathbf{H}_{2}$ adsorption capacities at RT and 1 atm. The gravimetric uptake is the highest among all MOFs reported for $\mathbf{C}_{2}\mathbf{H}_{2}$ adsorption, while the volumetric one is only lower than those of HKUST-1 and MOF-74 series. As established in HKUST-1 in which each open copper site binds one $\mathbf{C}_{2}\mathbf{H}_{2}$ molecule, the copper site density of $3.45\mathrm{\mmol\g}^{-1}$ in NOTT-109 can contribute $77.4~\mathrm{cm}^{3}$ storage capacity of the total $204~\mathrm{cm}^{3}$ at 1 atm and $296\mathrm{~K~}$ The remaining $\mathbf{C}_{2}\mathbf{H}_{2}$ can be stored inside the pore space of moderate sizes within NOTT-109.
The $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ storage properties of some porous MOFs constructed from the organic linkers containing $m$ -benzenedicarboxylate units are summarized in Table 5. The well investigated MOFs HKUST-1 and NiMOF-74 were also included. It can be seen that a few MOFs exhibit excellent methane adsorption properties. For example, at RT and 65 bar, NOTT-101, NOTT-102, NOTT-103, and NU-125 show the methane uptakes over $230\ \mathrm{cm}^{3}\left(\mathrm{STP}\right)\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ and the deliverable amounts higher than $190~\mathrm{cm}^{3}$ (STP) $\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ . At RT and 35 bar, NOTT-101 and NOTT-103 exhibit the methane uptakes higher than $190~\mathrm{cm}^{3}$ (STP) $\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ and the deliverable amounts higher than $135~\mathrm{cm}^{3}$ (STP) $\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ . To date, HKUST-1 is the best-performing MOF material for volumetric $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ storage and delivery.
Table 6 lists the $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ adsorption capacities at low and high pressures for some porous MOFs constructed from the organic linkers containing $m$ -benzenedicarboxylate units. PCN-12, UTSA-20, and SNU-5 have $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ uptakes over $2.8~\mathrm{wt\%}$ at $77\mathrm{~K~}$ and 1 bar. The high pressure $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ adsorption capacities of some MOFs have surpassed $10\mathrm{\wt{\%}}$ at $77\ \mathrm{\K}.$ These MOFs include NJU-Bai10, PCN-68, NOTT-112, NOTT-119, and NU-100. Apparently, the higher is the porosity of the MOFs, the higher is its $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ storage capacity under high pressure and $77\mathrm{K}.$ Those MOFs with small pore spaces, specific binding sites and moderate surface areas are favourable to their high $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ uptake at $77~\mathrm{K}$ and 1 bar.
We also summarize in Table 7 the $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption capacities of some porous MOFs constructed from the organic linkers containing $m$ -benzenedicarboxylate units. Again, their high pressure saturated $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption capacities are linearly related to their porosities (BET surface areas), while those exhibiting high $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ uptakes at RT and 1 atm, as demonstrated in PCN-26, Cu-TDPAT and NOTT-122, Cu-TPBTM, and $\mathbf{Cu}_{2}(\mathbf{dbip})$ , need to have specific sites for their enhanced interactions with $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ molecules.
7. Recent development in the exploration of expanded $m$ -benzenedicarboxylate units for the construction of highly porous MOFs
Due to the power of the basic $m$ -benzenedicarboxylate organic building subunits to construct highly porous MOFs, as discussed above, several expanded versions of $m$ -benzenedicarboxylate units shown in Scheme 5 were developed. For example, Kong et al.182 synthesized two tricarboxylate organic linkers $\mathrm{\bf\ddot{H}}_{3}\mathrm{\bfD}1$ and $\mathrm{\bfH}_{3}\mathrm{\bfD}2$ , see Scheme 5) containing the expanded organic building subunits II and III, which are self-assembled with dicopper paddlewheel $\mathrm{{Cu}_{2}(\mathrm{{COO})_{4}}}$ SBUs to form two highly porous MOFs ZJU-35 and ZJU-36, respectively. Their structures are isoreticular with HKUST-1, although the two linkers adopted are less symmetrical than $\mathbf{H}_{3}\mathbf{b}\mathrm{tc}$ . The expanded ligands resulted in the enlarged pores. High-pressure gas sorption isotherms indicate that both MOFs can take up a large amount of $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ at RT, and thus exhibit high potential for high-pressure swing adsorption (PSA) purification of $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ .
$C_{3}$ -symmetrical tri-isophthalates are assembled with paddlewheel units to usually form a (3,24)-connected rht-based network, which is based on 3-connected MBBs and 24-connected cuboctahedral SBBs. The latter consist of 12 paddlewheel units joined by 24 isophthalates. To enlarge the cuboctahedral SBB, Eddaoudi et al.183 synthesized an organic linker $\mathrm{\Delta}\mathrm{\cdot}\mathrm{H}_{6}\mathrm{D}3$ , Scheme 5) incorporating the expanded organic building subunits IV in which longer benzoate moieties replace the carboxylates of $m$ -benzenedicarboxylate, and constructed the corresponding (3,24)-connected rht-based networks. The expanded isophthalate evidently enlarged SBBs with a diameter of $25.7\mathring{\mathbf{A}}$ , almost double the size of the original SBB built from isophthalate moieties (Fig. 31).
It should be mentioned that in addition to m-benzenedicarboxylate subunits, carbazole-3,6-dicarboxyaltes featuring a $90^{\circ}$ -bridging angle between carboxylate groups are being explored as new organic building subunits (V, Scheme 5). Zhou et al.184,185 designed the organic linkers $\mathrm{\Delta[H_{8}D4}$ , $\mathbf{H}_{4}\mathbf{D}5$ and $\mathrm{H}_{4}\mathrm{D}6$ , Scheme 5) incorporating such organic building subunits, and realized highly porous MOFs PCN-80, PCN-81 and PCN-82, respectively. It is well known that 12 carbazole-3,6-dicarboxylate ligands and 6 dicopper paddlewheel clusters are easily assembled into a discrete octahedral cage (Fig. 31).186 Such octahedral cages serving as 12-conencted nodes were observed in PCN-81 and PCN-82, but not in PCN-80.
Evidently, the incorporation of these new organic building subunits with rich organic aromatic backbones will certainly lead to an abundance of new organic linkers for construction of highly porous MOFs in the near future.
## 8. Conclusion and outlook
The development of new organic linkers is still very important to target functional MOF materials. Among the diverse basic units, the meta-benzenedicarboxylate one, summarized in this review, is particularly of importance to be developed into a number of organic linkers and thus MOFs of diverse structures and tuneable pores. The tuneable structures and porosities within this series of synthesized MOFs have enabled them to have wide applications on gas storage, gas separation, heterogeneous catalysis and sensing. The fact that some MOFs summarized here exhibit extraordinarily high gas storage/capture capacities for $\mathbf{H}_{2}.$ ， $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ , $\mathbf{C}_{2}\mathbf{H}_{2}$ and $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ indicates that a few very promising ones might be practically implemented in our daily lives in the near future. The richness of organic backbones will not only enrich the database of organic linkers containing metabenzenedicarboxylate units, but will also lead to some even better MOFs for the above mentioned applications. Future research will also be focused on the self-assembly of other metal ions/metal clusters with the developed organic linkers from meta-benzenedicarboxylate units to construct porous MOFs and then to explore their diverse functions. Of course, as mentioned in Section 7, other types of expanded $m$ -benzenedicarboxylate units will also be extensively explored for their construction of functional MOFs in the near future.
